@@@ set-clear-toggle-bits
title: Set, Clear, Toggle and Test a Bit
difficulty: E
category: Bit Manipulation
order: 1
## Problem
Write four functions that operate on a 32-bit register value: `bit_set`, `bit_clear`, `bit_toggle` and `bit_test`. Each takes the value and a bit position `n` (0–31). The first three return the new value; `bit_test` returns `true` if bit `n` is set.

This is the most common opening question in firmware interviews. Write it without hesitation and explain why you use `1u`.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

uint32_t bit_set(uint32_t v, unsigned n)    { return v; }
uint32_t bit_clear(uint32_t v, unsigned n)  { return v; }
uint32_t bit_toggle(uint32_t v, unsigned n) { return v; }
bool     bit_test(uint32_t v, unsigned n)   { return false; }

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    CHECK(bit_set(0, 0) == 1u);
    CHECK(bit_set(0, 31) == 0x80000000u);
    CHECK(bit_clear(0xFFu, 3) == 0xF7u);
    CHECK(bit_toggle(0x10u, 4) == 0u);
    CHECK(bit_toggle(0u, 7) == 0x80u);
    CHECK(bit_test(0x8u, 3));
    CHECK(!bit_test(0x8u, 2));
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
Build a mask with a single 1 at position `n` by shifting `1u`. OR sets, AND with the inverted mask clears, XOR toggles.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

uint32_t bit_set(uint32_t v, unsigned n)    { return v | (1u << n); }
uint32_t bit_clear(uint32_t v, unsigned n)  { return v & ~(1u << n); }
uint32_t bit_toggle(uint32_t v, unsigned n) { return v ^ (1u << n); }
bool     bit_test(uint32_t v, unsigned n)   { return (v >> n) & 1u; }
```
## Explanation
Using `1u` makes the shift unsigned. With a plain `1`, `1 << 31` shifts into the sign bit of a signed `int`, which is undefined behaviour before C23. Shifting by 32 or more is undefined for any type, so production code often adds `n &= 31` or an assertion.

**Follow-ups:** How would you do this on a real register shared with an ISR? (Critical section, or atomic set/reset registers like STM32 `BSRR`.) What about a multi-bit field? See *Extract and Insert a Register Field*.

**Walk-through with x = 0b1010, n = 1**

| operation | mask | result |
|---|---|---|
| set | `1u << 1` = 0b0010 | `x | mask` = 0b1010 (already set) |
| clear | `~(1u << 1)` = …1101 | `x & ~mask` = 0b1000 |
| toggle | 0b0010 | `x ^ mask` = 0b1000 |
| test | 0b0010 | `(x >> 1) & 1u` = 1 → true |

**Common mistakes:** `1 << 31` on a signed int (undefined before C23 — use `1u`); `x & 1 << n` without parentheses (works here, but `x & 1 << n == 0` does not, because `==` binds tighter than `&`); testing with `x & (1u << n) == 1`, which is false for any bit except bit 0.

**Interview follow-ups:** do it on a hardware register (read-modify-write races with interrupts → use the peripheral's set/clear registers or a critical section); set or clear several bits at once with a mask; write the macros `BIT(n)`, `SET_BITS(reg, mask)` with proper parentheses.

**Complexity:** O(1), one or two instructions each.

@@@ count-set-bits
title: Count the Set Bits
difficulty: E
category: Bit Manipulation
order: 2
## Problem
Implement `popcount(uint32_t x)` that returns the number of 1 bits in `x`. Your loop should run once per **set** bit, not once per bit.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

unsigned popcount(uint32_t x)
{
    return 0;
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    CHECK(popcount(0) == 0);
    CHECK(popcount(1) == 1);
    CHECK(popcount(0xFFu) == 8);
    CHECK(popcount(0x80000001u) == 2);
    CHECK(popcount(0xFFFFFFFFu) == 32);
    CHECK(popcount(0xA5A5A5A5u) == 16);
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
What does `x & (x - 1)` do to the lowest set bit?
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

unsigned popcount(uint32_t x)
{
    unsigned n = 0;
    while (x) {
        x &= x - 1;   /* clear lowest set bit */
        n++;
    }
    return n;
}
```
## Explanation
Subtracting 1 flips the lowest set bit to 0 and all bits below it to 1; ANDing with the original clears exactly that bit. The loop runs once per set bit (Brian Kernighan's algorithm), so it is fast for sparse values.

For constant time, use the SWAR (parallel bit count) method, or a 16-entry nibble lookup table. In real code, `__builtin_popcount` lets the compiler pick the best instruction sequence for the target.

**Walk-through for x = 0b101100 (3 bits set)**

| iteration | x before | x & (x − 1) | count |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | 101100 | 101000 | 1 |
| 2 | 101000 | 100000 | 2 |
| 3 | 100000 | 000000 | 3 |

**Comparison of methods**

| Method | Work | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Test every bit | 32 iterations | simplest |
| Kernighan `x &= x − 1` | one iteration per set bit | asked here |
| Nibble table (16 entries) | 8 lookups | small table, constant time |
| SWAR (parallel adds) | ~12 operations | constant time, no table |
| `__builtin_popcount` / `CNT` (NEON) | 1–few instructions | best in production |

**Uses:** parity (`popcount & 1`), Hamming distance (`popcount(a ^ b)`), counting free blocks in a bitmap, counting active channels.

**Follow-up:** popcount of 64-bit values and arrays — use `__builtin_popcountll`, or sum per-byte counts with a 256-entry table.

@@@ reverse-bits-byte
title: Reverse the Bits of a Byte
difficulty: E
category: Bit Manipulation
order: 3
## Problem
Write `uint8_t reverse8(uint8_t b)` that mirrors the bit order: bit 0 becomes bit 7, bit 1 becomes bit 6, and so on. For example `0x01` becomes `0x80` and `0xB0` becomes `0x0D`.

This comes up when a peripheral sends LSB-first data but your protocol expects MSB-first.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

uint8_t reverse8(uint8_t b)
{
    return b;
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    CHECK(reverse8(0x01) == 0x80);
    CHECK(reverse8(0x80) == 0x01);
    CHECK(reverse8(0xB0) == 0x0D);
    CHECK(reverse8(0xFF) == 0xFF);
    CHECK(reverse8(0x00) == 0x00);
    for (unsigned i = 0; i < 256; i++) CHECK(reverse8(reverse8((uint8_t)i)) == i);
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
Swap the two nibbles, then swap pairs of bits within each nibble, then swap adjacent bits.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

uint8_t reverse8(uint8_t b)
{
    b = (uint8_t)((b & 0xF0u) >> 4 | (b & 0x0Fu) << 4);
    b = (uint8_t)((b & 0xCCu) >> 2 | (b & 0x33u) << 2);
    b = (uint8_t)((b & 0xAAu) >> 1 | (b & 0x55u) << 1);
    return b;
}
```
## Explanation
Three swap stages of width 4, 2 and 1 reverse 8 bits in constant time with no loop. The masks select the upper and lower half of each group. The casts back to `uint8_t` matter because of integer promotion: the expressions are computed as `int`.

Alternatives: a loop that shifts bits out of `b` and into the result (8 iterations), or a 256-byte lookup table in flash for the fastest speed. On ARMv7-M, `__RBIT(x) >> 24` does it in one instruction.

**Walk-through of the swap method for b = 0xB0 (1011 0000)**

1. Swap nibbles: 0000 1011 (0x0B)
2. Swap bit pairs within each nibble: 00 00 10 11 → 00 00 11 10 (0x0E)
3. Swap adjacent bits: 0000 1110 → 0000 1101 (0x0D) ✓

Each step uses two masks and two shifts: three steps for 8 bits, five for 32 bits — log₂(width) steps instead of a loop over every bit.

**Alternatives**

| Method | Cost |
|---|---|
| Loop over 8 bits (shift out, shift in) | 8 iterations, tiny code |
| 256-byte lookup table | 1 load, 256 bytes of flash |
| 16-entry nibble table | 2 loads + shift |
| Arm `RBIT` instruction (`__RBIT` intrinsic) | 1 instruction for 32 bits (`>> 24` for a byte) |

**Where it is used:** SPI/serial peripherals that only shift LSB first, CRC algorithms with reflected input/output, FFT bit-reversed addressing, and displays wired with reversed data lines.

@@@ swap-nibbles-endian
title: Swap Byte Order of a 32-bit Value
difficulty: E
category: Bit Manipulation
order: 4
## Problem
Write `uint32_t bswap32(uint32_t x)` that reverses the byte order (`0x11223344` becomes `0x44332211`), and `uint32_t load_be32(const uint8_t *p)` that reads a big-endian 32-bit value from a byte buffer, correctly on any host and at any alignment.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

uint32_t bswap32(uint32_t x) { return x; }
uint32_t load_be32(const uint8_t *p) { return 0; }

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    CHECK(bswap32(0x11223344u) == 0x44332211u);
    CHECK(bswap32(0x000000FFu) == 0xFF000000u);
    CHECK(bswap32(bswap32(0xDEADBEEFu)) == 0xDEADBEEFu);
    uint8_t buf[] = { 0x00, 0xDE, 0xAD, 0xBE, 0xEF };
    CHECK(load_be32(buf + 1) == 0xDEADBEEFu);   /* unaligned */
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
Move each byte to its new position with shifts and masks. For `load_be32`, build the value byte by byte instead of casting the pointer.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

uint32_t bswap32(uint32_t x)
{
    return  (x >> 24) |
           ((x >> 8) & 0x0000FF00u) |
           ((x << 8) & 0x00FF0000u) |
            (x << 24);
}

uint32_t load_be32(const uint8_t *p)
{
    return (uint32_t)p[0] << 24 | (uint32_t)p[1] << 16 |
           (uint32_t)p[2] << 8  | (uint32_t)p[3];
}
```
## Explanation
Casting `buf + 1` to `uint32_t *` would be both an unaligned access (HardFault on Cortex-M0) and a strict-aliasing violation, and it would still give the wrong answer on a little-endian host. Assembling from bytes is portable and the compiler turns it into `LDR` + `REV` where the hardware allows.

Note the casts to `uint32_t` before shifting: `p[0]` promotes to `int`, and `0xDE << 24` overflows a signed int.

**Walk-through of `bswap32(0x11223344)`**

| term | value |
|---|---|
| `x << 24` | 0x44000000 |
| `(x << 8) & 0x00FF0000` | 0x00330000 |
| `(x >> 8) & 0x0000FF00` | 0x00002200 |
| `x >> 24` | 0x00000011 |
| OR of all | **0x44332211** |

**Why `load_be32` uses shifts on bytes:** `(p[0] << 24) | (p[1] << 16) | (p[2] << 8) | p[3]` gives the same result on little- and big-endian hosts and never performs an unaligned 32-bit access, so it is safe on Cortex-M0 and with packed protocol buffers. Casting `p` to `uint32_t *` would be both endian-dependent and potentially a HardFault or strict-aliasing violation. Cast `p[0]` to `uint32_t` before shifting by 24, otherwise the promoted `int` can overflow.

**In production:** `__builtin_bswap32` (one `REV` instruction on Arm), `htonl`/`ntohl` for network order, and Linux `be32_to_cpu` / `get_unaligned_be32`.

**Interview follow-ups:** detect host endianness at run time (`*(uint8_t *)&(uint32_t){1}` is 1 on little-endian), swap 16-bit values (`REV16`), and write `store_le32`.

@@@ register-field
title: Extract and Insert a Register Field
difficulty: M
category: Bit Manipulation
order: 5
## Problem
Hardware registers pack several fields into one word. Write:

- `field_get(reg, shift, width)`: returns the `width`-bit field starting at bit `shift`.
- `field_set(reg, shift, width, val)`: returns `reg` with that field replaced by `val`, leaving every other bit unchanged. Bits of `val` that do not fit in the field must be ignored.

Both must work for `width == 32`.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

uint32_t field_get(uint32_t reg, unsigned shift, unsigned width)
{
    return 0;
}

uint32_t field_set(uint32_t reg, unsigned shift, unsigned width, uint32_t val)
{
    return reg;
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    CHECK(field_get(0x0000AB00u, 8, 8) == 0xABu);
    CHECK(field_get(0xF0000000u, 28, 4) == 0xFu);
    CHECK(field_get(0x12345678u, 0, 32) == 0x12345678u);
    CHECK(field_set(0xFFFFFFFFu, 4, 4, 0x0u) == 0xFFFFFF0Fu);
    CHECK(field_set(0x00000000u, 4, 4, 0x1Fu) == 0x000000F0u);   /* extra bits dropped */
    CHECK(field_set(0xDEADBEEFu, 0, 32, 0x1u) == 0x1u);
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
Build the mask without ever computing `1u << 32`. `0xFFFFFFFFu >> (32 - width)` gives `width` ones, as long as `width` is at least 1.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

static inline uint32_t ones(unsigned width)
{
    return width ? (0xFFFFFFFFu >> (32u - width)) : 0u;
}

uint32_t field_get(uint32_t reg, unsigned shift, unsigned width)
{
    return (reg >> shift) & ones(width);
}

uint32_t field_set(uint32_t reg, unsigned shift, unsigned width, uint32_t val)
{
    uint32_t mask = ones(width) << shift;
    return (reg & ~mask) | ((val << shift) & mask);
}
```
## Explanation
The textbook `(1u << width) - 1` breaks for `width == 32` because shifting by the full type width is undefined. Shifting the all-ones value right avoids that.

When applied to a real register this is a read-modify-write. If an ISR or another core can modify the same register, protect it with a critical section, or use hardware set/clear registers. In the Linux kernel, the same job is done by `FIELD_GET()` and `FIELD_PREP()` with `GENMASK()`.

**Walk-through: `field_set(0xFFFF0000, 4, 4, 0xA)`**

1. `mask = ((1u << 4) − 1) << 4` = 0x000000F0
2. Clear the field: `0xFFFF0000 & ~0xF0` = 0xFFFF0000
3. Insert: `(0xA << 4) & 0xF0` = 0xA0
4. Result: **0xFFFF00A0**

`field_get(0xFFFF00A0, 4, 4)` = `(0xFFFF00A0 >> 4) & 0xF` = 0xA.

**The width-32 trap:** `1u << 32` is undefined behaviour, so a full-width field needs a special case (`width == 32 ? 0xFFFFFFFFu : (1u << width) − 1u`) — that is what the tests check.

**Why mask `val` before inserting:** an oversized value (e.g. 0x1A into a 4-bit field) would otherwise overwrite neighbouring bits; masking silently truncates it (some APIs assert instead).

**Real-world equivalents:** CMSIS `_Pos`/`_Msk` macros, `MODIFY_REG(reg, clear_mask, set_mask)` on STM32, Linux `FIELD_PREP(GENMASK(7, 4), val)` and `FIELD_GET`.

**Hardware caution:** on an actual register this is a read-modify-write — protect it from interrupts that write the same register, and never use it on write-1-to-clear registers.

@@@ safe-string-copy
title: Safe String Copy (strlcpy)
difficulty: E
category: Pointers & Strings
order: 6
## Problem
Implement `size_t my_strlcpy(char *dst, const char *src, size_t size)`:

- Copy at most `size - 1` characters and **always** NUL-terminate `dst` when `size > 0`.
- Return the length of `src` (so the caller can detect truncation when the return value is `>= size`).
- Do not use any library string functions.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <stddef.h>

size_t my_strlcpy(char *dst, const char *src, size_t size)
{
    return 0;
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    char b[8];
    CHECK(my_strlcpy(b, "hi", sizeof b) == 2 && strcmp(b, "hi") == 0);
    CHECK(my_strlcpy(b, "overflowing", sizeof b) == 11 && strcmp(b, "overflo") == 0);
    CHECK(my_strlcpy(b, "", sizeof b) == 0 && b[0] == '\0');
    b[0] = 'X';
    CHECK(my_strlcpy(b, "abc", 0) == 3 && b[0] == 'X');   /* size 0: write nothing */
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
Copy while there is room, then keep counting the rest of `src` without copying to compute the return value.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <stddef.h>

size_t my_strlcpy(char *dst, const char *src, size_t size)
{
    const char *s = src;
    if (size) {
        while (--size && *s)
            *dst++ = *s++;
        *dst = '\0';
    }
    while (*s) s++;            /* count the remainder */
    return (size_t)(s - src);
}
```
## Explanation
`strncpy` is not a safe replacement for `strcpy`: it does not terminate the destination when the source is too long, and it zero-pads the whole buffer, which wastes time. `strlcpy` semantics (BSD, and now in glibc 2.38 and POSIX 2024) fix both. Returning the full source length lets callers write `if (my_strlcpy(d, s, n) >= n) /* truncated */`.

Edge cases interviewers check: `size == 0`, empty source, and exact-fit strings.

**Walk-through**

| call | copied | dst | return | truncated? |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `my_strlcpy(d, "hi", 8)` | `h i \0` | "hi" | 2 | no (2 < 8) |
| `my_strlcpy(d, "hello", 4)` | `h e l \0` | "hel" | 5 | yes (5 ≥ 4) |
| `my_strlcpy(d, "x", 0)` | nothing | unchanged | 1 | yes |

**Why return the source length:** the caller can check `if (ret >= size)` to detect truncation and, if needed, allocate `ret + 1` bytes and retry.

**Compared with the standard alternatives**

| Function | Always terminates | Detects truncation | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `strcpy` | yes | no | no bounds check at all |
| `strncpy` | **no** (if src ≥ n) | no | zero-pads, slow for big buffers |
| `snprintf(d, n, "%s", s)` | yes (n > 0) | yes (return value) | heavier, available everywhere |
| `strlcpy` | yes | yes | BSD, glibc 2.38+, Linux kernel uses `strscpy` |

**Complexity:** O(len(src)) — it scans the whole source to compute the return value even when copying less. Linux's `strscpy` returns `-E2BIG` instead and stops at the buffer size, which avoids reading a huge or unterminated source.

@@@ implement-memmove
title: Implement memmove
difficulty: M
category: Pointers & Strings
order: 7
## Problem
Implement `void *my_memmove(void *dst, const void *src, size_t n)` that copies `n` bytes correctly even when the source and destination regions overlap.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <stddef.h>

void *my_memmove(void *dst, const void *src, size_t n)
{
    return dst;
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    char a[] = "123456789";
    my_memmove(a + 2, a, 5);                 /* overlap, dst after src */
    CHECK(memcmp(a, "121234589", 9) == 0);
    char b[] = "123456789";
    my_memmove(b, b + 2, 5);                 /* overlap, dst before src */
    CHECK(memcmp(b, "345676789", 9) == 0);
    char c[4] = "abc", d[4] = { 0 };
    CHECK(my_memmove(d, c, 4) == d && strcmp(d, "abc") == 0);
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
If the destination starts after the source and they overlap, copying forwards overwrites source bytes before you read them. Copy backwards in that case.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <stddef.h>
#include <stdint.h>

void *my_memmove(void *dst, const void *src, size_t n)
{
    unsigned char *d = dst;
    const unsigned char *s = src;

    if (d == s || n == 0)
        return dst;
    if ((uintptr_t)d < (uintptr_t)s || (uintptr_t)d >= (uintptr_t)s + n) {
        while (n--) *d++ = *s++;            /* forward copy is safe */
    } else {
        d += n; s += n;
        while (n--) *--d = *--s;            /* copy backwards */
    }
    return dst;
}
```
## Explanation
Comparing unrelated pointers with `<` is technically unspecified in C, which is why the comparison is done on `uintptr_t`. Real library versions copy word by word once both pointers are aligned, which is much faster for large buffers.

**Follow-up:** why is `memcpy` allowed to be faster? Because its `restrict` parameters let it assume no overlap and copy in any order, including with SIMD or DMA.

**Walk-through: shift right by 2 inside one buffer**

`buf = "ABCDE__"`, `my_memmove(buf + 2, buf, 5)`: the destination starts inside the source range, so copy backwards: E → buf[6], D → buf[5], C → buf[4], B → buf[3], A → buf[2] → `"ABABCDE"`. Copying forwards would read A, B, then the already-overwritten A, B again → `"ABABABA"`.

**Decision rule**

| condition | direction |
|---|---|
| `dst <= src` or `dst >= src + n` | forward |
| `src < dst < src + n` | backward |

**Why not use a temporary buffer:** it needs O(n) extra memory (stack or heap), which may not exist on an MCU, and doubles the copying.

**Edge cases tested:** `n == 0`, `dst == src`, full overlap in both directions, and non-overlapping copies.

**Performance follow-up:** copy word-sized chunks after aligning, in either direction (the backward loop aligns from the end). Comparing unrelated pointers with `<` is technically unspecified in C; converting to `uintptr_t` (as libraries do) avoids the issue.

@@@ atoi-overflow
title: String to Integer with Error Checking
difficulty: M
category: Pointers & Strings
order: 8
## Problem
Write `bool parse_int32(const char *s, int32_t *out)` that converts a decimal string to an `int32_t`.

- Accept an optional leading `+` or `-`.
- Reject empty strings, strings with any non-digit characters, and values outside `INT32_MIN..INT32_MAX`.
- On success write the value to `*out` and return `true`; on failure return `false` and leave `*out` unchanged.

You would use something like this to parse commands from a UART console.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

bool parse_int32(const char *s, int32_t *out)
{
    return false;
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    int32_t v = 7;
    CHECK(parse_int32("123", &v) && v == 123);
    CHECK(parse_int32("-42", &v) && v == -42);
    CHECK(parse_int32("+5", &v) && v == 5);
    CHECK(parse_int32("2147483647", &v) && v == INT32_MAX);
    CHECK(parse_int32("-2147483648", &v) && v == INT32_MIN);
    v = 7;
    CHECK(!parse_int32("2147483648", &v) && v == 7);
    CHECK(!parse_int32("", &v));
    CHECK(!parse_int32("-", &v));
    CHECK(!parse_int32("12a", &v));
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
Accumulate the magnitude as an unsigned 32-bit (or 64-bit) value and check the limit **before** each multiply-and-add. The negative limit is one larger than the positive one.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

bool parse_int32(const char *s, int32_t *out)
{
    bool neg = false;
    uint32_t mag = 0;

    if (*s == '+' || *s == '-') neg = (*s++ == '-');
    if (*s == '\0') return false;

    const uint32_t limit = neg ? 2147483648u : 2147483647u;
    for (; *s; s++) {
        if (*s < '0' || *s > '9') return false;
        uint32_t d = (uint32_t)(*s - '0');
        if (mag > (limit - d) / 10u) return false;   /* mag*10 + d > limit */
        mag = mag * 10u + d;
    }
    *out = neg ? (int32_t)(0u - mag) : (int32_t)mag;
    return true;
}
```
## Explanation
`atoi` cannot report errors at all, and signed overflow during accumulation is undefined behaviour, so the magnitude is kept unsigned. The overflow test is rearranged so it never overflows itself. Converting `0u - mag` back to `int32_t` for `INT32_MIN` relies on two's complement conversion, which is guaranteed in C23 and is how every real compiler behaves.

The standard-library alternative is `strtol` with `errno` and `endptr` checks.

**The overflow check without overflowing**

Accumulate in a wider or in the negative range. One clean way: accumulate the magnitude in `uint32_t` / `int64_t` and check against the limit **before** each multiply-add:

- positive limit 2,147,483,647, negative limit 2,147,483,648 (one larger in magnitude).
- `if (acc > (limit − digit) / 10) → overflow`.

**Walk-through**

| input | result |
|---|---|
| `"123"` | 123, true |
| `"-2147483648"` | INT32_MIN, true (the asymmetric limit) |
| `"2147483648"` | false (one too large) |
| `"+"`, `""`, `"12a"`, `" 12"` | false (no digits / invalid characters — this API does not skip spaces) |

**Common mistakes**

- Computing `acc * 10 + d` first and checking afterwards — signed overflow is undefined, so the check may be optimised away.
- Building a positive number and negating it: `-(2147483648)` does not fit in `int32_t`.
- Accepting an empty string or a lone sign as 0.
- Using `atoi`, which has no error reporting at all; `strtol` with `errno` and `endptr` checks is the standard alternative.

**Why it matters in firmware:** parsing configuration values and commands from UART, BLE or a web interface — unvalidated numbers are a common source of crashes and security bugs.

@@@ reverse-linked-list
title: Reverse a Singly Linked List
difficulty: E
category: Data Structures
order: 9
## Problem
Given the head of a singly linked list, reverse it in place and return the new head. Use O(1) extra memory and no recursion.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stddef.h>

struct node { int val; struct node *next; };

struct node *reverse(struct node *head)
{
    return head;
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    struct node n[5];
    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) { n[i].val = i; n[i].next = (i < 4) ? &n[i + 1] : NULL; }
    struct node *h = reverse(&n[0]);
    int expect = 4, count = 0;
    for (struct node *p = h; p; p = p->next, expect--, count++) CHECK(p->val == expect);
    CHECK(count == 5);
    CHECK(reverse(NULL) == NULL);
    struct node one = { 9, NULL };
    CHECK(reverse(&one) == &one && one.next == NULL);
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
Keep three pointers: the previous node, the current node and the next node (saved before you overwrite `current->next`).
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stddef.h>

struct node { int val; struct node *next; };

struct node *reverse(struct node *head)
{
    struct node *prev = NULL;
    while (head) {
        struct node *next = head->next;
        head->next = prev;
        prev = head;
        head = next;
    }
    return prev;
}
```
## Explanation
Each step detaches the current node and pushes it onto the front of the reversed list held in `prev`. O(n) time, O(1) space. A recursive solution is shorter but uses O(n) stack, which is unacceptable on a small MCU stack for long lists.

**Follow-ups:** reverse in groups of k; reverse a doubly linked list (swap `next` and `prev` in each node); detect a cycle first.

**Walk-through for 1 → 2 → 3**

| step | prev | cur | next saved | list state |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| start | NULL | 1 | — | 1 → 2 → 3 |
| 1 | 1 | 2 | 2 | NULL ← 1 2 → 3 |
| 2 | 2 | 3 | 3 | NULL ← 1 ← 2 3 |
| 3 | 3 | NULL | NULL | NULL ← 1 ← 2 ← 3 |

Return `prev` (node 3).

**The one rule:** save `cur->next` **before** overwriting it, or the rest of the list is lost.

**Edge cases tested:** empty list (returns NULL), single node (returns itself), two nodes.

**Why not recursion:** the recursive version is elegant but uses one stack frame per node; on an MCU with a 1 KB task stack, a few hundred nodes can overflow it.

**Follow-ups:** reverse between positions m and n, reverse in groups of k, check whether the list is a palindrome (reverse the second half), reverse a doubly linked list (swap `next` and `prev` in every node).

**Complexity:** O(n) time, O(1) extra space.

@@@ ring-buffer
title: Lock-Free Ring Buffer (SPSC)
difficulty: M
category: Data Structures
order: 10
## Problem
Implement a byte FIFO for a UART receive path where the ISR is the only producer and the main loop is the only consumer.

- Capacity is `RB_SIZE` (a power of two) and **all** `RB_SIZE` slots must be usable.
- `rb_put` returns `false` when full; `rb_get` returns `false` when empty.
- `rb_count` returns the number of stored bytes.
- No locks and no disabling interrupts.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

#define RB_SIZE 8u   /* must be a power of two */

typedef struct {
    uint8_t buf[RB_SIZE];
    volatile uint32_t head;   /* written only by producer */
    volatile uint32_t tail;   /* written only by consumer */
} ring_t;

void     rb_init(ring_t *r)               { (void)r; }
bool     rb_put(ring_t *r, uint8_t b)     { return false; }
bool     rb_get(ring_t *r, uint8_t *b)    { return false; }
uint32_t rb_count(const ring_t *r)        { return 0; }

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    ring_t r; uint8_t b;
    rb_init(&r);
    CHECK(rb_count(&r) == 0 && !rb_get(&r, &b));
    for (unsigned i = 0; i < RB_SIZE; i++) CHECK(rb_put(&r, (uint8_t)i));
    CHECK(!rb_put(&r, 99) && rb_count(&r) == RB_SIZE);
    CHECK(rb_get(&r, &b) && b == 0);
    CHECK(rb_put(&r, 100));
    for (unsigned i = 1; i < RB_SIZE; i++) CHECK(rb_get(&r, &b) && b == i);
    CHECK(rb_get(&r, &b) && b == 100 && rb_count(&r) == 0);
    /* wrap-around of the free-running counters */
    r.head = r.tail = 0xFFFFFFFEu;
    for (unsigned i = 0; i < 5; i++) CHECK(rb_put(&r, (uint8_t)i));
    for (unsigned i = 0; i < 5; i++) CHECK(rb_get(&r, &b) && b == i);
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
Let `head` and `tail` count up forever instead of wrapping them at `RB_SIZE`. Then `head - tail` (unsigned) is always the fill level, and you index the array with `index & (RB_SIZE - 1)`.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

#define RB_SIZE 8u

typedef struct {
    uint8_t buf[RB_SIZE];
    volatile uint32_t head;
    volatile uint32_t tail;
} ring_t;

#define COMPILER_BARRIER() __asm__ volatile("" ::: "memory")

void rb_init(ring_t *r) { r->head = r->tail = 0; }

uint32_t rb_count(const ring_t *r) { return r->head - r->tail; }

bool rb_put(ring_t *r, uint8_t b)
{
    uint32_t h = r->head;
    if (h - r->tail == RB_SIZE) return false;   /* full */
    r->buf[h & (RB_SIZE - 1u)] = b;
    COMPILER_BARRIER();                         /* data before index */
    r->head = h + 1u;
    return true;
}

bool rb_get(ring_t *r, uint8_t *b)
{
    uint32_t t = r->tail;
    if (r->head == t) return false;             /* empty */
    *b = r->buf[t & (RB_SIZE - 1u)];
    COMPILER_BARRIER();                         /* read data before freeing slot */
    r->tail = t + 1u;
    return true;
}
```
## Explanation
Each index has exactly one writer, and each update is a single aligned 32-bit store, which is atomic on a 32-bit MCU. The producer stores the byte before publishing the new `head`, and the consumer reads it before publishing the new `tail`, so neither side sees a half-finished slot.

Free-running counters solve the classic full-versus-empty ambiguity without wasting a slot, and unsigned subtraction stays correct when the counters wrap past `0xFFFFFFFF` (the test checks this).

On a multi-core or out-of-order CPU, replace the compiler barrier with real ordering: C11 `atomic_store_explicit(&head, h + 1, memory_order_release)` in the producer and an acquire load in the consumer.

**Why free-running indices use all slots:** `head` and `tail` only ever increase; the number of stored bytes is `head − tail` (unsigned, wrap-safe), so full is `count == RB_SIZE` and empty is `count == 0` — no slot has to stay empty to tell them apart. The array position is `index & (RB_SIZE − 1)`.

**Walk-through (RB_SIZE = 4)**

| op | head | tail | count | result |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| put ×4 | 4 | 0 | 4 | full |
| put | 4 | 0 | 4 | false |
| get | 4 | 1 | 3 | first byte |
| put | 5 | 1 | 4 | stored in slot 5 & 3 = 0 |

Wrap-around of the 32-bit counters after 4 billion bytes is harmless: unsigned subtraction still gives the right count.

**ISR safety:** the ISR writes only `head`, the main loop writes only `tail`; on a single-core MCU, `volatile` indices with the data written **before** `head` is updated are enough. On Cortex-M7 or multi-core, use C11 atomics with release/acquire ordering.

**Follow-ups:** what if two ISRs write (needs a critical section), overwrite-oldest mode, bulk `rb_read(buf, n)` with two `memcpy` calls around the wrap, and DMA-fed circular buffers.

@@@ memory-pool
title: Fixed-Block Memory Pool
difficulty: M
category: Data Structures
order: 11
## Problem
Many firmware projects forbid `malloc`. Implement a pool of `POOL_BLOCKS` blocks of `BLOCK_SIZE` bytes from a static array:

- `pool_init()` prepares the pool.
- `pool_alloc()` returns a free block in O(1), or `NULL` if none remain.
- `pool_free(p)` returns a block in O(1).

Use no memory beyond the static array itself (no separate bitmap).
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stddef.h>
#include <stdint.h>

#define BLOCK_SIZE  32u
#define POOL_BLOCKS 4u

void  pool_init(void)    { }
void *pool_alloc(void)   { return NULL; }
void  pool_free(void *p) { (void)p; }

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    pool_init();
    void *b[POOL_BLOCKS];
    for (unsigned i = 0; i < POOL_BLOCKS; i++) { b[i] = pool_alloc(); CHECK(b[i] != NULL); }
    CHECK(pool_alloc() == NULL);
    for (unsigned i = 0; i < POOL_BLOCKS; i++)
        for (unsigned j = i + 1; j < POOL_BLOCKS; j++) CHECK(b[i] != b[j]);
    CHECK(((uintptr_t)b[0] % sizeof(void *)) == 0);
    pool_free(b[2]);
    CHECK(pool_alloc() == b[2]);
    pool_free(b[0]); pool_free(b[1]);
    CHECK(pool_alloc() != NULL && pool_alloc() != NULL && pool_alloc() == NULL);
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
A free block is not in use, so you can store a "next free block" pointer inside it. Chain all free blocks into a singly linked free list.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stddef.h>
#include <stdint.h>

#define BLOCK_SIZE  32u
#define POOL_BLOCKS 4u

typedef union block {
    union block *next;                 /* valid while free */
    unsigned char data[BLOCK_SIZE];    /* valid while allocated */
} block_t;

static block_t  pool[POOL_BLOCKS];
static block_t *free_list;

void pool_init(void)
{
    for (unsigned i = 0; i < POOL_BLOCKS - 1u; i++)
        pool[i].next = &pool[i + 1];
    pool[POOL_BLOCKS - 1u].next = NULL;
    free_list = &pool[0];
}

void *pool_alloc(void)
{
    block_t *b = free_list;
    if (b) free_list = b->next;
    return b;
}

void pool_free(void *p)
{
    if (!p) return;
    block_t *b = p;
    b->next = free_list;
    free_list = b;
}
```
## Explanation
The union guarantees each block is large enough and aligned for a pointer. Allocation pops the head of the free list and freeing pushes onto it, both O(1) and deterministic, with no fragmentation because every block is the same size. This is the idea behind RTOS memory pools (ThreadX block pools, Zephyr `k_mem_slab`).

For use from both tasks and ISRs, wrap `alloc` and `free` in a critical section. Debug builds often add a magic value or a range check in `pool_free` to catch double frees and foreign pointers.

**How the free list lives inside the free blocks:** each free block's first bytes store a pointer to the next free block, so no extra memory is needed. `pool_alloc` pops the head of the list; `pool_free` pushes the block back. Both are O(1) and deterministic.

**Walk-through (3 blocks: A, B, C)**

```
init:          free → A → B → C → NULL
alloc() → A    free → B → C
alloc() → B    free → C
free(A)        free → A → C
alloc() → A    (last freed is reused first)
```

**Requirements the tests check:** `BLOCK_SIZE` must be at least `sizeof(void *)` and blocks must be aligned for the data stored in them (`_Alignas(max_align_t)` on the array, or round the block size up to 8); `pool_alloc` returns NULL when exhausted.

**Robustness extras interviewers like:** detect double free and foreign pointers (range check, `(p − base) % BLOCK_SIZE == 0`, a used/free bitmap), keep a usage high-water mark, fill freed blocks with a pattern to catch use-after-free, and protect with a critical section if ISRs allocate.

**Why pools beat malloc in firmware:** no fragmentation, bounded time, and failures happen only when the pool is genuinely exhausted — which you can size from measurements.

@@@ moving-average
title: Moving Average Filter
difficulty: E
category: Signal Processing
order: 12
## Problem
Implement a moving average over the last `N` ADC samples (12-bit values in a `uint16_t`). `ma_add(x)` adds a sample and returns the current average. Until `N` samples have arrived, average over the samples received so far. Each call must be O(1).
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

#define N 4u

uint16_t ma_add(uint16_t x)
{
    return x;
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    CHECK(ma_add(100) == 100);
    CHECK(ma_add(200) == 150);
    CHECK(ma_add(300) == 200);
    CHECK(ma_add(400) == 250);
    CHECK(ma_add(500) == 350);     /* window is 200..500 */
    CHECK(ma_add(4095) == 1323);   /* (300+400+500+4095)/4 = 1323 */
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
Keep a running sum. On each new sample subtract the value leaving the window and add the new one.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

#define N 4u

uint16_t ma_add(uint16_t x)
{
    static uint16_t win[N];
    static uint32_t sum;
    static uint32_t idx, count;

    sum -= win[idx];       /* zero until the window fills */
    sum += x;
    win[idx] = x;
    idx = (idx + 1u) % N;
    if (count < N) count++;
    return (uint16_t)(sum / count);
}
```
## Explanation
The running sum makes each update O(1) regardless of window size. A `uint32_t` sum cannot overflow for 12-bit samples unless N exceeds about one million. If N is a power of two, the division becomes a shift once the window is full.

Alternatives: an exponential moving average `avg += (x - avg) >> k` uses no buffer, which is popular on small MCUs; a median filter rejects spikes better than an average.

**Walk-through (N = 4)**

| sample | buffer | sum | count | average |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 100 | 100 | 100 | 1 | 100 |
| 200 | 100 200 | 300 | 2 | 150 |
| 300 | 100 200 300 | 600 | 3 | 200 |
| 400 | 100 200 300 400 | 1000 | 4 | 250 |
| 500 | 500 200 300 400 | 1400 | 4 | 350 (100 dropped) |

**O(1) trick:** keep a running sum — subtract the sample being overwritten, add the new one — instead of summing N values each time.

**Integer details:** 12-bit samples (max 4095) × N must fit in the sum type (`uint32_t` is fine for N up to a million); with N a power of two, `sum >> log2(N)` replaces the division; add `count / 2` before dividing to round instead of truncating.

**Startup behaviour:** averaging over `count` samples until the buffer is full avoids a slow ramp from zero (which the tests check).

**Alternatives:** exponential moving average `y += (x − y) >> k` (no buffer, one subtraction and shift), median filter for spikes, and proper FIR/IIR filters for defined frequency responses.

@@@ debounce-button
title: Debounce a Push Button
difficulty: M
category: Drivers & Peripherals
order: 13
## Problem
A button is sampled every 1 ms by a timer ISR that calls `debounce(raw)`, where `raw` is the current pin level (1 = pressed). The function should return:

- `1` exactly once when the button becomes **stably pressed** (raw is 1 for 5 consecutive samples),
- `-1` exactly once when it becomes stably released (5 consecutive 0s),
- `0` otherwise.

Bounces shorter than 5 samples must be ignored. The initial state is released.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

int debounce(int raw)
{
    return 0;
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
static int feed(const char *s)   /* returns sum of events, counts presses*10 */
{
    int presses = 0, releases = 0;
    for (; *s; s++) { int e = debounce(*s == '1'); if (e == 1) presses++; if (e == -1) releases++; }
    return presses * 10 + releases;
}
int main(void)
{
    CHECK(feed("0000") == 0);
    CHECK(feed("1010110") == 0);            /* bouncing, no event */
    CHECK(feed("11111") == 10);             /* one press */
    CHECK(feed("1111111111") == 0);         /* held: no repeat */
    CHECK(feed("0100100") == 0);            /* short glitches while held */
    CHECK(feed("00000") == 1);              /* release */
    CHECK(feed("1111100000") == 11);
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
Keep the current stable state and a counter. Count samples that differ from the stable state; reset the counter whenever a sample agrees with it.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

#define STABLE_SAMPLES 5u

int debounce(int raw)
{
    static int stable;        /* 0 = released, 1 = pressed */
    static unsigned cnt;

    if (raw == stable) {
        cnt = 0;              /* agrees with current state */
        return 0;
    }
    if (++cnt >= STABLE_SAMPLES) {
        stable = raw;
        cnt = 0;
        return stable ? 1 : -1;
    }
    return 0;
}
```
## Explanation
This is a two-state machine with a counter. Any sample matching the stable state resets the count, so a glitch has to last the full 5 ms to register. Events are edge-triggered, which is what application code usually wants ("button pressed" rather than "button is down").

A compact alternative keeps an 8-bit shift-register history and checks for `0x1F`/`0xE0` patterns. Avoid debouncing with edge interrupts alone: a bouncing contact can fire dozens of interrupts. Five milliseconds suits most tactile switches; check the switch datasheet.

**Walk-through (threshold 5 samples)**

| raw samples | stable state | return |
|---|---|---|
| 1 0 1 1 0 (bounce) | released | 0 each time |
| 1 1 1 1 1 | → pressed on the 5th | **1** once |
| 1 1 1 … | pressed | 0 |
| 0 0 0 0 0 | → released on the 5th | **−1** once |

The counter resets whenever the raw level matches the current stable state, so only five **consecutive** different samples change the state, and events are reported only on the transition.

**Choosing the time:** mechanical switches bounce for roughly 1–20 ms; 5 samples at 1 ms (5 ms) suits most tactile switches, 20–50 ms for cheap or large switches.

**Alternatives**

| Method | Notes |
|---|---|
| Counter (this solution) | simple, symmetric press/release |
| Shift-register history (`hist = (hist << 1) | raw`; pressed when `hist == 0xFF`) | elegant, 8-sample window |
| Interrupt on edge + timer to re-check after 10 ms | low power, no constant sampling |
| Hardware RC filter + Schmitt trigger | no firmware, costs parts |

**Follow-ups:** long-press and double-click detection (a small state machine with timestamps), debouncing many buttons at once with bit-parallel "vertical counters", and why sampling must be periodic (from a timer, not the main loop).

@@@ wrap-safe-timeout
title: Wrap-Safe Timeout Check
difficulty: E
category: Drivers & Peripherals
order: 14
## Problem
A 32-bit millisecond tick counter overflows every 49.7 days. Write `bool timeout_expired(uint32_t now, uint32_t start, uint32_t timeout_ms)` that returns `true` once at least `timeout_ms` have passed since `start`, and stays correct across the counter wrapping from `0xFFFFFFFF` to `0`.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

bool timeout_expired(uint32_t now, uint32_t start, uint32_t timeout_ms)
{
    return now >= start + timeout_ms;   /* buggy! */
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    CHECK(!timeout_expired(1000, 900, 200));
    CHECK(timeout_expired(1100, 900, 200));
    CHECK(!timeout_expired(0xFFFFFFF0u, 0xFFFFFF00u, 0x200u));
    CHECK(!timeout_expired(0x00000050u, 0xFFFFFF00u, 0x200u));   /* wrapped, 0x150 elapsed */
    CHECK(timeout_expired(0x00000100u, 0xFFFFFF00u, 0x200u));    /* wrapped, 0x200 elapsed */
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
Compute the elapsed time first, using unsigned subtraction.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

bool timeout_expired(uint32_t now, uint32_t start, uint32_t timeout_ms)
{
    return (uint32_t)(now - start) >= timeout_ms;
}
```
## Explanation
Unsigned arithmetic is modulo 2³², so `now - start` is the true elapsed time even if `now` has wrapped past zero, provided the real interval is shorter than 2³² ms. The buggy version fails because `start + timeout_ms` itself overflows to a small number, making the timeout expire immediately (or never).

The `(uint32_t)` cast matters on 8- and 16-bit targets where narrower types would be promoted to signed `int`. Linux uses `time_after(a, b)` macros on `jiffies` for exactly this reason.

**Why `now − start >= timeout` works across the wrap**

| now | start | `now − start` (uint32) | elapsed |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1000 | 900 | 100 | 100 ms |
| 50 | 0xFFFFFFCE (−50) | 100 | 100 ms across the wrap ✓ |

Unsigned subtraction is modulo 2³², which is exactly the elapsed time as long as it is less than 2³² ms (49.7 days).

**The broken version:** `now >= start + timeout` — if `start + timeout` wraps to a small number, the condition is true immediately; if `now` wraps before reaching it, it may take 49 days to become true.

**Equivalent signed form for deadlines:** `(int32_t)(now − deadline) >= 0` tests whether a precomputed deadline has passed, valid while the timeout is under 2³¹ ms (24.8 days). Linux uses this in `time_after(a, b)`.

**Testing tip:** start your tick counter near 0xFFFFFFFF in debug builds (Linux starts jiffies 5 minutes before the wrap) so wrap bugs appear in the first minutes rather than after weeks in the field.

@@@ crc16-ccitt
title: CRC-16/CCITT-FALSE
difficulty: M
category: Protocols & Parsing
order: 15
## Problem
Implement `uint16_t crc16_ccitt(const uint8_t *data, size_t len)` using polynomial `0x1021`, initial value `0xFFFF`, no input/output reflection and no final XOR (the "CCITT-FALSE" variant). The standard check value for the ASCII string `"123456789"` is `0x29B1`.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stddef.h>
#include <string.h>

uint16_t crc16_ccitt(const uint8_t *data, size_t len)
{
    return 0;
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    const char *s = "123456789";
    CHECK(crc16_ccitt((const uint8_t *)s, strlen(s)) == 0x29B1);
    CHECK(crc16_ccitt(NULL, 0) == 0xFFFF);
    const uint8_t a = 'A';
    CHECK(crc16_ccitt(&a, 1) == 0xB915);
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
XOR each byte into the **top** 8 bits of the CRC, then shift left 8 times; whenever the bit shifted out was 1, XOR in the polynomial.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stddef.h>
#include <string.h>

uint16_t crc16_ccitt(const uint8_t *data, size_t len)
{
    uint16_t crc = 0xFFFFu;
    while (len--) {
        crc ^= (uint16_t)(*data++) << 8;
        for (int i = 0; i < 8; i++)
            crc = (crc & 0x8000u) ? (uint16_t)((crc << 1) ^ 0x1021u) : (uint16_t)(crc << 1);
    }
    return crc;
}
```
## Explanation
This is polynomial long division in GF(2), processed MSB first. It costs 8 iterations per byte. A 256-entry `uint16_t` table (512 bytes of flash) processes a byte in one lookup: `crc = (crc << 8) ^ table[(crc >> 8) ^ byte]`. Many MCUs (STM32, nRF) include a hardware CRC unit.

There are dozens of "CRC-16" variants differing in polynomial, initial value, reflection and final XOR. Always confirm the parameters against a check value like the one in the test.

**How the bitwise loop works:** XOR the next data byte into the top 8 bits of the 16-bit CRC, then process 8 bits: if the top bit is 1, shift left and XOR with the polynomial 0x1021; otherwise just shift left. This is long division in GF(2), keeping only the remainder.

**Check value:** for the ASCII string `"123456789"`, CCITT-FALSE gives **0x29B1** — always verify an implementation against the catalogue check value, because CRC "names" are ambiguous.

**Parameters that define a CRC-16**

| Variant | Init | Reflect in/out | Final XOR | Check |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| CCITT-FALSE (this one) | 0xFFFF | no | 0 | 0x29B1 |
| XMODEM | 0x0000 | no | 0 | 0x31C3 |
| KERMIT | 0x0000 | yes | 0 | 0x2189 |
| MODBUS (poly 0x8005) | 0xFFFF | yes | 0 | 0x4B37 |

**Faster versions:** a 256-entry table (512 bytes of flash) processes a byte per lookup: `crc = (crc << 8) ^ table[(crc >> 8) ^ byte]`; a 16-entry nibble table is a good compromise; many MCUs have a configurable CRC peripheral (polynomial, init, reflection).

**What CRC-16 detects:** all single- and double-bit errors, all odd numbers of bit errors (this polynomial), and all bursts up to 16 bits — but it is not a security check; anyone can forge a matching CRC.

@@@ frame-parser
title: UART Packet Parser State Machine
difficulty: H
category: Protocols & Parsing
order: 16
## Problem
A sensor sends packets over UART in this format:

| Field | Size | Value |
|---|---|---|
| Start | 1 | `0xAA` |
| Length | 1 | payload length, 1–16 |
| Payload | Length | data |
| Checksum | 1 | XOR of Length and every payload byte |

Write `int parser_feed(uint8_t byte)`, called once per received byte. It returns the payload length when a valid packet completes (copying the payload into the global `rx_payload`), and 0 otherwise. Bad lengths and checksum errors must be dropped silently, and the parser must resynchronise on the next `0xAA`.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <string.h>

uint8_t rx_payload[16];

int parser_feed(uint8_t byte)
{
    return 0;
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
static int feed_all(const uint8_t *b, size_t n)
{
    int last = 0;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) { int r = parser_feed(b[i]); if (r) last = r; }
    return last;
}
int main(void)
{
    const uint8_t ok[]   = { 0xAA, 3, 0x10, 0x20, 0x30, 3 ^ 0x10 ^ 0x20 ^ 0x30 };
    CHECK(feed_all(ok, sizeof ok) == 3 && memcmp(rx_payload, ok + 2, 3) == 0);
    const uint8_t bad[]  = { 0xAA, 2, 0x01, 0x02, 0x00 };                     /* wrong checksum */
    CHECK(feed_all(bad, sizeof bad) == 0);
    const uint8_t junk[] = { 0x00, 0x55, 0xAA, 0x40, 0xAA, 1, 0x7E, 1 ^ 0x7E }; /* bad len, then resync */
    CHECK(feed_all(junk, sizeof junk) == 1 && rx_payload[0] == 0x7E);
    const uint8_t aa_in_payload[] = { 0xAA, 2, 0xAA, 0xAA, 2 };
    CHECK(feed_all(aa_in_payload, sizeof aa_in_payload) == 2);
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
Use an `enum` of states: waiting for start, waiting for length, receiving payload, waiting for checksum. Keep the running XOR and the index as static variables.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <string.h>

uint8_t rx_payload[16];

typedef enum { ST_START, ST_LEN, ST_DATA, ST_CSUM } pstate_t;

int parser_feed(uint8_t byte)
{
    static pstate_t st = ST_START;
    static uint8_t len, idx, csum;

    switch (st) {
    case ST_START:
        if (byte == 0xAA) st = ST_LEN;
        break;
    case ST_LEN:
        if (byte >= 1 && byte <= sizeof rx_payload) {
            len = byte; idx = 0; csum = byte;
            st = ST_DATA;
        } else {
            st = (byte == 0xAA) ? ST_LEN : ST_START;   /* resync */
        }
        break;
    case ST_DATA:
        rx_payload[idx++] = byte;
        csum ^= byte;
        if (idx == len) st = ST_CSUM;
        break;
    case ST_CSUM:
        st = ST_START;
        if (byte == csum) return len;
        break;
    }
    return 0;
}
```
## Explanation
A byte-at-a-time state machine never blocks, works directly from a UART ISR or ring buffer, and handles packets split across reads. Validating the length before storing anything prevents a buffer overflow from a corrupted length byte, which is a classic security bug.

Improvements for production: copy the payload to the application only after the checksum passes (here a failing packet partially overwrites `rx_payload`), add an inter-byte timeout to reset the state, and use a CRC instead of XOR. Because `0xAA` can appear in the payload, real protocols often use **byte stuffing** (SLIP, COBS, HDLC) so the start marker is unique.

**State machine**

```
WAIT_START --0xAA--> GET_LEN --1..16--> GET_PAYLOAD (len bytes) --> GET_CHECKSUM --ok--> deliver, WAIT_START
     ^                  |invalid length                               |bad checksum
     +------------------+---------------------------------------------+
```

**Walk-through: `AA 02 10 20 22`**

| byte | state | action |
|---|---|---|
| AA | WAIT_START | → GET_LEN |
| 02 | GET_LEN | len = 2, chk = 0x02 |
| 10 | GET_PAYLOAD | chk ^= 0x10 → 0x12 |
| 20 | GET_PAYLOAD | chk ^= 0x20 → 0x32 |
| 22 | GET_CHECKSUM | 0x22 ≠ 0x32 → error, back to WAIT_START |

(With checksum byte 0x32 the frame would be accepted.)

**Robustness points the tests check:** invalid lengths (0 or > 16) are rejected immediately instead of waiting for many bytes; a corrupted frame does not desynchronise the parser — it returns to hunting for 0xAA; one byte at a time, so it can run from a UART ISR or a ring-buffer reader.

**Improvements for real protocols:** an inter-byte timeout that resets the parser if a frame stalls, a CRC instead of XOR (XOR misses many errors, e.g. two bytes with the same bit flipped), byte stuffing (SLIP/COBS) so the start byte cannot appear inside the payload, and sequence numbers to detect lost frames.

@@@ nmea-checksum
title: Validate an NMEA Sentence
difficulty: M
category: Protocols & Parsing
order: 17
## Problem
GPS modules output NMEA 0183 sentences like:

```
$GPGGA,123519,4807.038,N,01131.000,E,1,08,0.9,545.4,M,46.9,M,,*47
```

The checksum is the XOR of every character between `$` and `*` (exclusive), written as two hex digits after `*`. Write `bool nmea_valid(const char *s)` that returns `true` only if the sentence starts with `$`, contains `*` followed by exactly two hex digits (upper or lower case) and an optional `\r\n`, and the checksum matches.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdbool.h>
#include <stdint.h>

bool nmea_valid(const char *s)
{
    return false;
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    CHECK(nmea_valid("$GPGGA,123519,4807.038,N,01131.000,E,1,08,0.9,545.4,M,46.9,M,,*47"));
    CHECK(nmea_valid("$GPGGA,123519,4807.038,N,01131.000,E,1,08,0.9,545.4,M,46.9,M,,*47\r\n"));
    CHECK(!nmea_valid("$GPGGA,123519,4807.038,N,01131.000,E,1,08,0.9,545.4,M,46.9,M,,*48"));
    CHECK(nmea_valid("$A*41"));
    CHECK(nmea_valid("$a*61"));
    CHECK(!nmea_valid("GPGGA*00"));
    CHECK(!nmea_valid("$A*4"));
    CHECK(!nmea_valid("$A41"));
    CHECK(!nmea_valid("$A*4G"));
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
Write a small helper that converts one hex character to its value or returns -1.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdbool.h>
#include <stdint.h>

static int hexval(char c)
{
    if (c >= '0' && c <= '9') return c - '0';
    if (c >= 'A' && c <= 'F') return c - 'A' + 10;
    if (c >= 'a' && c <= 'f') return c - 'a' + 10;
    return -1;
}

bool nmea_valid(const char *s)
{
    if (*s++ != '$') return false;

    uint8_t sum = 0;
    while (*s && *s != '*') sum ^= (uint8_t)*s++;
    if (*s++ != '*') return false;

    int hi = hexval(s[0]);
    if (hi < 0) return false;
    int lo = hexval(s[1]);
    if (lo < 0) return false;
    s += 2;

    if (s[0] == '\r' && s[1] == '\n') s += 2;
    if (*s != '\0') return false;
    return sum == (uint8_t)(hi << 4 | lo);
}
```
## Explanation
The checks are ordered so the code never reads past the terminator: `s[1]` is only examined after `s[0]` was a valid hex digit, which means it was not `'\0'`. Parsing defensively matters because GPS data arrives over a noisy link and often starts mid-sentence.

In a real receiver you would accumulate characters from the UART ring buffer into a line buffer of bounded size (NMEA allows 82 characters), validate, and then split fields on commas.

**Walk-through for `$GPGLL,4916.45,N,12311.12,W,225444,A*1D`**

1. Start after `$`, XOR every character up to (not including) `*`.
2. Parse the two characters after `*` as hex (`1D` → 0x1D), accepting upper and lower case.
3. Valid if the computed XOR equals the parsed value.

**Validation cases the tests check:** missing `$`, missing `*`, fewer than two hex digits, invalid hex characters, a wrong checksum, and an optional trailing `\r\n`.

**Hex parsing without library calls**

```c
static int hexval(char c)
{
    if (c >= '0' && c <= '9') return c - '0';
    if (c >= 'A' && c <= 'F') return c - 'A' + 10;
    if (c >= 'a' && c <= 'f') return c - 'a' + 10;
    return -1;
}
```

**Context:** NMEA sentences arrive over UART at 9600–115200 baud from GPS modules; a typical receiver collects characters into a line buffer (bounded length, 82 characters max per NMEA), validates the checksum, then splits fields at commas. The XOR checksum only detects simple errors — it cannot detect two swapped characters — so treat field values carefully (range checks).

@@@ bitbang-spi
title: Bit-Banged SPI Transfer (Mode 0)
difficulty: M
category: Drivers & Peripherals
order: 18
## Problem
Implement `uint8_t spi_xfer(uint8_t out)` that sends and receives one byte, MSB first, in **SPI mode 0** (CPOL = 0, CPHA = 0) using three GPIO helper functions: `sck(int level)`, `mosi(int level)` and `miso()`. The clock idles low. The test harness simulates a peripheral that samples MOSI on the rising edge and changes MISO on the falling edge.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

/* ---- simulated hardware (do not modify) ---- */
static int pin_sck, pin_mosi;
static uint8_t dev_rx, dev_tx = 0x3C;
static int dev_bit = 7;
static void sck(int level)
{
    if (!pin_sck && level) dev_rx = (uint8_t)(dev_rx << 1 | pin_mosi);   /* rising: sample */
    if (pin_sck && !level && dev_bit > 0) dev_bit--;                      /* falling: next bit */
    pin_sck = level;
}
static void mosi(int level) { pin_mosi = level ? 1 : 0; }
static int  miso(void)      { return (dev_tx >> dev_bit) & 1; }
/* --------------------------------------------- */

uint8_t spi_xfer(uint8_t out)
{
    return 0;
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    uint8_t in = spi_xfer(0xA5);
    CHECK(dev_rx == 0xA5);
    CHECK(in == 0x3C);
    CHECK(pin_sck == 0);       /* clock idles low */
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
In mode 0, data must be valid **before** the rising edge. So for each bit: set MOSI, raise SCK, read MISO, lower SCK.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

static int pin_sck, pin_mosi;
static uint8_t dev_rx, dev_tx = 0x3C;
static int dev_bit = 7;
static void sck(int level)
{
    if (!pin_sck && level) dev_rx = (uint8_t)(dev_rx << 1 | pin_mosi);
    if (pin_sck && !level && dev_bit > 0) dev_bit--;
    pin_sck = level;
}
static void mosi(int level) { pin_mosi = level ? 1 : 0; }
static int  miso(void)      { return (dev_tx >> dev_bit) & 1; }

uint8_t spi_xfer(uint8_t out)
{
    uint8_t in = 0;
    for (int i = 7; i >= 0; i--) {
        mosi((out >> i) & 1);      /* set data while SCK is low */
        sck(1);                    /* rising edge: both sides sample */
        in = (uint8_t)(in << 1 | miso());
        sck(0);                    /* falling edge: both sides shift */
    }
    return in;
}
```
## Explanation
SPI is just two shift registers connected in a loop: each clock pulse sends one bit and receives one bit, so a transfer is always full duplex. In mode 0 both sides sample on the rising edge and change data on the falling edge; the first bit must therefore be on MOSI before the first rising edge.

On real hardware add delays to meet the device's maximum clock and setup/hold times, and toggle chip select around the transfer. For mode 1 you would shift on the rising edge and sample on the falling edge instead. Bit-banging is useful when you run out of hardware SPI blocks or need an odd frame length.

**Timing for one bit in mode 0 (CPOL = 0, CPHA = 0)**

```
MOSI: set bit 7 ──┐            set bit 6 ──┐
SCK:  ____________|‾‾‾‾‾‾‾‾|______________|‾‾‾‾‾‾‾|___
                  ↑ both sides sample on the rising edge
MISO: read here ──┘ (after raising SCK)
```

Per bit: put the output bit on MOSI while SCK is low, raise SCK (both sides sample), read MISO, lower SCK (the peripheral shifts out its next bit). Repeat 8 times, MSB first, shifting the received bit into the result.

**Walk-through: sending 0xA5 (1010 0101)** — MOSI goes 1,0,1,0,0,1,0,1 on successive clocks while the received byte is built as `in = (in << 1) | miso()`.

**Other modes**

| Mode | Change data | Sample |
|---|---|---|
| 0 | SCK low, before rising | rising |
| 1 | on rising | falling |
| 2 | SCK high, before falling | falling |
| 3 | on falling | rising |

**Real hardware notes:** add delays (or rely on GPIO speed) to respect the device's maximum SCK frequency and setup/hold times; control chip select around multi-byte transfers; use the GPIO set/reset registers for fast, atomic pin changes; bit-banging costs 100% CPU, so prefer the hardware SPI peripheral except when pins or modes are unusual.

@@@ reset-handler
title: Write the Reset Handler
difficulty: M
category: Startup & Boot
order: 19
## Problem
Before `main()` runs, start-up code must copy the `.data` section's initial values from flash to RAM and zero `.bss`. Implement `reset_init()` using the linker-provided symbols. In this simulation the "flash" and "RAM" are arrays, and the symbols are pointers into them.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <string.h>

/* ---- simulated linker symbols ---- */
static const uint32_t flash_image[3] = { 0x11111111u, 0x22222222u, 0x33333333u };
static uint32_t ram[8] = { 0xDEAD, 0xDEAD, 0xDEAD, 0xDEAD, 0xDEAD, 0xDEAD, 0xDEAD, 0xDEAD };
static const uint32_t *_sidata = flash_image;   /* load address of .data (flash) */
static uint32_t *_sdata = &ram[0], *_edata = &ram[3];   /* .data in RAM */
static uint32_t *_sbss  = &ram[3], *_ebss  = &ram[7];   /* .bss in RAM  */
/* ---------------------------------- */

void reset_init(void)
{
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    reset_init();
    CHECK(ram[0] == 0x11111111u && ram[1] == 0x22222222u && ram[2] == 0x33333333u);
    CHECK(ram[3] == 0 && ram[4] == 0 && ram[5] == 0 && ram[6] == 0);
    CHECK(ram[7] == 0xDEAD);   /* must not touch memory past _ebss */
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
Two loops over word pointers: copy from `_sidata` to `_sdata` until `_edata`, then write zero from `_sbss` until `_ebss`.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <string.h>

static const uint32_t flash_image[3] = { 0x11111111u, 0x22222222u, 0x33333333u };
static uint32_t ram[8] = { 0xDEAD, 0xDEAD, 0xDEAD, 0xDEAD, 0xDEAD, 0xDEAD, 0xDEAD, 0xDEAD };
static const uint32_t *_sidata = flash_image;
static uint32_t *_sdata = &ram[0], *_edata = &ram[3];
static uint32_t *_sbss  = &ram[3], *_ebss  = &ram[7];

void reset_init(void)
{
    const uint32_t *src = _sidata;
    for (uint32_t *dst = _sdata; dst < _edata; )
        *dst++ = *src++;
    for (uint32_t *dst = _sbss; dst < _ebss; )
        *dst++ = 0;
}
```
## Explanation
On real hardware the symbols come from the linker script, declared as `extern uint32_t _sdata, _edata, ...;` and used by address (`&_sdata`). The linker script aligns the sections to 4 bytes so word copies are safe.

A real reset handler then calls `SystemInit()` (clocks, FPU enable), `__libc_init_array()` for C++ constructors, and finally `main()`. It must not rely on any initialised global data before these loops run, and it must be careful not to let the compiler turn the loops into calls to `memcpy`/`memset` that themselves depend on initialised data (`-ffreestanding` or `-fno-builtin` help). Some projects write this in assembly for that reason.

**What the linker-script symbols mean**

| symbol | meaning |
|---|---|
| `_sidata` | load address of .data initial values (in flash) |
| `_sdata`, `_edata` | run address range of .data (in RAM) |
| `_sbss`, `_ebss` | range of .bss in RAM |

**Steps in a real reset handler**

1. (Optional) set the stack pointer, enable the FPU, configure clocks (`SystemInit`).
2. Copy `_edata − _sdata` bytes from `_sidata` to `_sdata`.
3. Zero `_sbss`..`_ebss`.
4. Call C++ constructors (`__libc_init_array`).
5. Call `main()`; if it returns, loop forever or reset.

**Why copy .data:** RAM contents are undefined after power-up, so initialised globals must get their values from flash at every boot. **Why zero .bss:** C guarantees zero for uninitialised statics; storing zeros in flash would waste space.

**Common bugs:** using `<=` instead of `<` at the end address (writes one word too far), copying bytes when the symbols are word-aligned (slower, but correct), forgetting to zero .bss (variables start with garbage, often only noticed after a warm reset), and calling functions that use globals before this initialisation (e.g. a clock-setup function that writes a global `SystemCoreClock`).

@@@ hardfault-handler
title: HardFault Handler that Captures the Stack Frame
difficulty: H
category: Startup & Boot
order: 20
## Problem
When a Cortex-M HardFault occurs, the hardware has pushed `R0, R1, R2, R3, R12, LR, PC, xPSR` (in that order) onto whichever stack was in use. Bit 2 of the `EXC_RETURN` value in `LR` tells you which: 0 means MSP, 1 means PSP.

Write the C part of the handler, `void fault_decode(uint32_t exc_return, uint32_t *msp, uint32_t *psp, fault_info_t *out)`, that selects the right stack and copies the stacked `pc`, `lr` and `xpsr` into `out`. Then sketch the naked assembly entry that would call it.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

typedef struct { uint32_t pc, lr, xpsr, r0; } fault_info_t;

void fault_decode(uint32_t exc_return, uint32_t *msp, uint32_t *psp, fault_info_t *out)
{
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    uint32_t main_stack[8]    = { 1, 2, 3, 4, 12, 0x08000101u, 0x08000200u, 0x01000000u };
    uint32_t process_stack[8] = { 9, 0, 0, 0, 0,  0x08000301u, 0x08000400u, 0x21000000u };
    fault_info_t f;
    fault_decode(0xFFFFFFF9u, main_stack, process_stack, &f);       /* thread mode, MSP */
    CHECK(f.pc == 0x08000200u && f.lr == 0x08000101u && f.r0 == 1);
    fault_decode(0xFFFFFFFDu, main_stack, process_stack, &f);       /* thread mode, PSP */
    CHECK(f.pc == 0x08000400u && f.xpsr == 0x21000000u && f.r0 == 9);
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
`exc_return & 4` selects the stack. In the frame, `PC` is at index 6, `LR` at 5 and `xPSR` at 7.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

typedef struct { uint32_t pc, lr, xpsr, r0; } fault_info_t;

enum { F_R0, F_R1, F_R2, F_R3, F_R12, F_LR, F_PC, F_XPSR };

void fault_decode(uint32_t exc_return, uint32_t *msp, uint32_t *psp, fault_info_t *out)
{
    const uint32_t *frame = (exc_return & 4u) ? psp : msp;
    out->r0   = frame[F_R0];
    out->lr   = frame[F_LR];
    out->pc   = frame[F_PC];
    out->xpsr = frame[F_XPSR];
}

/*
 * On target, the entry point is a naked function so the compiler
 * does not push anything before we read the stack pointers:
 *
 * __attribute__((naked)) void HardFault_Handler(void)
 * {
 *     __asm volatile(
 *         "mov   r0, lr      \n"   // EXC_RETURN
 *         "mrs   r1, msp     \n"
 *         "mrs   r2, psp     \n"
 *         "ldr   r3, =g_fault\n"
 *         "b     fault_decode_and_halt \n");
 * }
 */
```
## Explanation
The stacked PC is the address of the faulting instruction (for precise faults), so `addr2line -e firmware.elf 0x08000200` points straight at the line of code. The stacked LR shows the caller. Together with `SCB->CFSR`, `HFSR`, `MMFAR` and `BFAR`, this usually identifies the bug without a debugger attached.

Production handlers save this data to a no-init RAM region, trigger a reset, and report it on the next boot. If the FPU was active, bit 4 of `EXC_RETURN` is 0 and the frame is 26 words long, but the first 8 words are laid out the same way.

**Selecting the stack with EXC_RETURN**

| EXC_RETURN | meaning |
|---|---|
| 0xFFFFFFF9 | return to Thread mode, MSP (bit 2 = 0) |
| 0xFFFFFFFD | return to Thread mode, PSP (bit 2 = 1) — typical for RTOS tasks |
| 0xFFFFFFF1 | return to Handler mode (fault inside an ISR), MSP |
| 0xFFFFFFE9 / …ED | same, with an FPU frame stacked |

The frame at the selected stack pointer is `[R0, R1, R2, R3, R12, LR, PC, xPSR]`, so the faulting PC is word 6 and the caller's LR is word 5.

**Using the decoded values:** look up PC with `arm-none-eabi-addr2line -e app.elf 0x0800xxxx`; check `CFSR` (0xE000ED28) to see why (e.g. `PRECISERR` with `BFAR` = the bad address, `UNDEFINSTR`, `INVSTATE` for a function pointer without the Thumb bit, `STKERR` for stack overflow); `HFSR.FORCED` means a configurable fault escalated.

**In real firmware:** the assembly entry (`tst lr, #4; ite eq; mrseq r0, msp; mrsne r0, psp; b fault_decode`) passes the frame pointer to C; the C code saves the registers to a `.noinit` area, then resets, so the next boot can report the crash. Breakpoint (`BKPT`) only when a debugger is attached.

@@@ aligned-malloc
title: Aligned Allocation
difficulty: H
category: Pointers & Strings
order: 21
## Problem
DMA engines and caches often need buffers aligned to 32 or 64 bytes. Using only `malloc` and `free`, write:

- `void *aligned_alloc_x(size_t align, size_t size)`: returns memory aligned to `align` (a power of two), or `NULL`.
- `void aligned_free_x(void *p)`: frees memory from `aligned_alloc_x`, and accepts `NULL`.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <string.h>

void *aligned_alloc_x(size_t align, size_t size) { return NULL; }
void  aligned_free_x(void *p) { (void)p; }

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    size_t aligns[] = { 8, 16, 32, 64, 256, 4096 };
    for (size_t i = 0; i < sizeof aligns / sizeof aligns[0]; i++) {
        uint8_t *p = aligned_alloc_x(aligns[i], 100);
        CHECK(p != NULL);
        CHECK(((uintptr_t)p & (aligns[i] - 1)) == 0);
        memset(p, 0xAB, 100);       /* must own all 100 bytes */
        aligned_free_x(p);
    }
    CHECK(aligned_alloc_x(24, 10) == NULL);   /* not a power of two */
    aligned_free_x(NULL);
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
Over-allocate by `align - 1` bytes plus room for one pointer. Round the address up, and store the original `malloc` pointer just before the aligned block so `free` can find it.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <string.h>

void *aligned_alloc_x(size_t align, size_t size)
{
    if (align == 0 || (align & (align - 1)) != 0) return NULL;
    if (align < sizeof(void *)) align = sizeof(void *);

    size_t extra = align - 1 + sizeof(void *);
    if (size > SIZE_MAX - extra) return NULL;

    void *raw = malloc(size + extra);
    if (!raw) return NULL;

    uintptr_t p = ((uintptr_t)raw + sizeof(void *) + align - 1) & ~(uintptr_t)(align - 1);
    ((void **)p)[-1] = raw;           /* remember the original pointer */
    return (void *)p;
}

void aligned_free_x(void *p)
{
    if (p) free(((void **)p)[-1]);
}
```
## Explanation
Reserving `sizeof(void *)` bytes before rounding up guarantees there is always room for the hidden pointer, and since the alignment is at least pointer size, the hidden pointer slot is itself aligned. The size check prevents integer overflow in `size + extra`, a common security bug.

C11 provides `aligned_alloc(align, size)` (size must be a multiple of align) and POSIX provides `posix_memalign`; the Linux kernel's `kmalloc` gives natural alignment for power-of-two sizes. For DMA buffers on cached cores, also round the **size** up to a cache-line multiple so invalidation does not destroy neighbouring data.

**Walk-through (align = 64, malloc returns 0x1004, pointer size 8)**

1. Request `size + 63 + 8` bytes.
2. `start = 0x1004 + 8 = 0x100C`.
3. `aligned = (0x100C + 63) & ~63 = 0x1040`.
4. Store 0x1004 at `0x1038` (the word just before 0x1040); return 0x1040.
5. `aligned_free_x(0x1040)` reads 0x1004 from 0x1038 and calls `free(0x1004)`.

**Checks the tests expect:** non-power-of-two alignment → NULL; alignment smaller than a pointer is raised (or rejected); `size + extra` overflow → NULL; `aligned_free_x(NULL)` does nothing; the returned pointer is aligned and the full `size` bytes are usable.

**Why it matters:** DMA buffers on cores with caches must not share a cache line with other data (cache maintenance on the buffer would corrupt the neighbour), so they are aligned and sized to the cache line (32 bytes on Cortex-M7, 64 on most Cortex-A). Other uses: SIMD loads, page-aligned buffers for the MMU, hardware descriptor tables.

**Standard alternatives:** C11 `aligned_alloc` (size must be a multiple of the alignment), POSIX `posix_memalign`, and static buffers with `_Alignas(64)`.

@@@ q15-saturating-multiply
title: Q15 Fixed-Point Multiply with Saturation
difficulty: H
category: Signal Processing
order: 22
## Problem
Many MCUs lack an FPU, so DSP code uses **Q15** fixed point: an `int16_t` represents a value in [-1, 1) as `value × 32768`. Write:

- `int16_t q15_mul(int16_t a, int16_t b)`: the product, rounded to nearest, saturated to the Q15 range.
- `int16_t q15_add(int16_t a, int16_t b)`: the sum, saturated instead of wrapping.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

int16_t q15_mul(int16_t a, int16_t b) { return 0; }
int16_t q15_add(int16_t a, int16_t b) { return 0; }

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
#define Q15(x) ((int16_t)((x) * 32768.0))
int main(void)
{
    CHECK(q15_mul(Q15(0.5), Q15(0.5)) == Q15(0.25));
    CHECK(q15_mul(Q15(-0.5), Q15(0.5)) == Q15(-0.25));
    CHECK(q15_mul(INT16_MIN, INT16_MIN) == INT16_MAX);   /* -1 * -1 saturates */
    CHECK(q15_mul(INT16_MIN, INT16_MAX) == -32767);
    CHECK(q15_add(Q15(0.75), Q15(0.5)) == INT16_MAX);
    CHECK(q15_add(Q15(-0.75), Q15(-0.5)) == INT16_MIN);
    CHECK(q15_add(100, -50) == 50);
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
Multiply in 32 bits: the product of two Q15 numbers is Q30. Add half an LSB (`1 << 14`) for rounding, shift right by 15, then clamp to `INT16_MIN..INT16_MAX`.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

static inline int16_t sat16(int32_t x)
{
    if (x > INT16_MAX) return INT16_MAX;
    if (x < INT16_MIN) return INT16_MIN;
    return (int16_t)x;
}

int16_t q15_mul(int16_t a, int16_t b)
{
    int32_t p = (int32_t)a * b;          /* Q30 */
    p += 1 << 14;                         /* round to nearest */
    return sat16(p >> 15);                /* back to Q15 */
}

int16_t q15_add(int16_t a, int16_t b)
{
    return sat16((int32_t)a + b);
}
```
## Explanation
The only product that overflows Q15 is −1 × −1 = +1, which cannot be represented, so it saturates to 0x7FFF. Saturation is preferred over wrap-around in signal processing because wrapping turns a slightly too-loud sample into a huge negative spike, which is audible and can destabilise control loops.

`p >> 15` on a negative value relies on arithmetic right shift (implementation-defined but universal on GCC/Clang/Arm compilers). Cortex-M4 and later provide `QADD`, `SSAT` and `SMULBB` instructions (exposed through CMSIS intrinsics and CMSIS-DSP's `arm_mult_q15`) that do this in a cycle or two.

**Q15 basics:** an `int16_t` value v represents v / 32768, so 0x4000 = 0.5, 0x7FFF ≈ 0.99997, 0x8000 = −1.0.

**Multiplication walk-through: 0.5 × 0.5**

1. `0x4000 * 0x4000 = 0x1000_0000` (a Q30 result in 32 bits).
2. Add rounding: `+ (1 << 14)`.
3. Shift right by 15 → 0x2000 = 0.25 ✓

**The one overflow case:** −1 × −1 = +1, which Q15 cannot represent; `0x8000 * 0x8000 >> 15` = 0x8000 (−1) without saturation. Clamp the result to 0x7FFF.

**Saturating add:** compute in 32 bits, then clamp to [−32768, 32767]. Without saturation, 0.75 + 0.5 wraps to a negative number — in an audio or control loop, a wrap produces a loud click or a violent actuator jump, while saturation just clips.

**On real hardware:** Cortex-M4/M7 DSP instructions do this in one cycle: `QADD16`, `SMULBB`, `SSAT`, `QDADD`; CMSIS-DSP provides `arm_mult_q15`, `arm_add_q15`. Right-shifting a negative number is implementation-defined in C (arithmetic on all common compilers) — mention it.

**Other formats:** Q31 for higher precision, Q7 for neural networks on MCUs (CMSIS-NN); choose a format by the value range and the precision you need.

@@@ lookup-interpolation
title: Calibration Table Interpolation
difficulty: M
category: Signal Processing
order: 23
## Problem
A thermistor is calibrated with a table of (ADC reading, temperature in 0.1 °C) points, sorted by ADC value. Write `int16_t adc_to_temp(uint16_t adc)` that:

- finds the two table entries surrounding `adc` with a **binary search**,
- linearly interpolates between them using integer arithmetic only,
- clamps to the first or last temperature when `adc` is outside the table.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

typedef struct { uint16_t adc; int16_t temp; } cal_t;
static const cal_t table[] = {
    {  500,  850 }, { 1000,  600 }, { 1800,  350 }, { 2600,  150 }, { 3400, -100 },
};
#define TABLE_LEN (sizeof table / sizeof table[0])

int16_t adc_to_temp(uint16_t adc)
{
    return 0;
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    CHECK(adc_to_temp(100) == 850);       /* below table */
    CHECK(adc_to_temp(500) == 850);
    CHECK(adc_to_temp(750) == 725);       /* halfway 850..600 */
    CHECK(adc_to_temp(1800) == 350);
    CHECK(adc_to_temp(2200) == 250);
    CHECK(adc_to_temp(3000) == 25);       /* 150 + (-250 * 400 / 800) */
    CHECK(adc_to_temp(4095) == -100);     /* above table */
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
Binary-search for the first entry whose `adc` is ≥ the input. Then interpolate with `t0 + (t1 - t0) * (x - x0) / (x1 - x0)` using 32-bit signed math.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

typedef struct { uint16_t adc; int16_t temp; } cal_t;
static const cal_t table[] = {
    {  500,  850 }, { 1000,  600 }, { 1800,  350 }, { 2600,  150 }, { 3400, -100 },
};
#define TABLE_LEN (sizeof table / sizeof table[0])

int16_t adc_to_temp(uint16_t adc)
{
    if (adc <= table[0].adc) return table[0].temp;
    if (adc >= table[TABLE_LEN - 1].adc) return table[TABLE_LEN - 1].temp;

    size_t lo = 1, hi = TABLE_LEN - 1;          /* first index with table[i].adc >= adc */
    while (lo < hi) {
        size_t mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
        if (table[mid].adc < adc) lo = mid + 1;
        else hi = mid;
    }
    const cal_t *a = &table[lo - 1], *b = &table[lo];
    int32_t dt = (int32_t)b->temp - a->temp;
    int32_t dx = (int32_t)b->adc - a->adc;
    return (int16_t)(a->temp + dt * (int32_t)(adc - a->adc) / dx);
}
```
## Explanation
A piecewise-linear table in flash is how most firmware linearises non-linear sensors (thermistors, flow sensors, battery discharge curves), because it is far cheaper than evaluating the Steinhart-Hart equation with floating point. Binary search keeps lookup O(log n), which matters for large tables; for evenly spaced ADC points you can compute the index directly.

Watch for overflow in `dt * (adc - a->adc)` when tables have large ranges, and note that integer division truncates towards zero. Add `dx / 2` with the right sign for rounding if needed.

**Walk-through:** table points (1000, 250) and (1200, 200) — ADC 1000 = 25.0 °C, ADC 1200 = 20.0 °C. For adc = 1050:

`temp = 250 + (1050 − 1000) × (200 − 250) / (1200 − 1000) = 250 − 12.5 = 237.5` → 238 with rounding (23.8 °C) or 237 with truncation, so round deliberately.

**Steps**

1. Clamp: below the first entry return the first temperature, above the last return the last (avoid extrapolating).
2. Binary search for the last index `i` with `table[i].adc <= adc` — O(log n).
3. Interpolate between `i` and `i + 1` with 32-bit intermediates (the product can exceed 16 bits).

**Integer arithmetic details:** do the multiplication before the division to keep precision; use signed types because the temperature slope is negative for NTC thermistors; add half the divisor for rounding (with care for the sign).

**Why a table instead of the formula:** the Steinhart–Hart equation needs logarithms and floating point; a 16–33 point table with linear interpolation is fast, accurate enough (better than ±0.1 °C with good point placement) and can include per-board calibration.

**Uses beyond thermistors:** battery voltage → state of charge, gamma correction, non-linear sensors (pressure, flow), and motor control lookup tables.

@@@ run-length-encoding
title: Run-Length Encoding
difficulty: M
category: Algorithms
order: 24
## Problem
Implement `size_t rle_encode(const uint8_t *in, size_t n, uint8_t *out, size_t cap)` that writes pairs of (count, value) bytes. Runs longer than 255 are split. Return the number of bytes written, or 0 if `out` is too small (never write past `cap`).

RLE is used to compress display bitmaps, fonts and log data on small devices.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <string.h>

size_t rle_encode(const uint8_t *in, size_t n, uint8_t *out, size_t cap)
{
    return 0;
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    uint8_t out[16];
    const uint8_t a[] = { 7, 7, 7, 1, 2, 2 };
    const uint8_t ea[] = { 3, 7, 1, 1, 2, 2 };
    CHECK(rle_encode(a, sizeof a, out, sizeof out) == 6 && memcmp(out, ea, 6) == 0);
    uint8_t big[300]; memset(big, 0x55, sizeof big);
    const uint8_t eb[] = { 255, 0x55, 45, 0x55 };
    CHECK(rle_encode(big, sizeof big, out, sizeof out) == 4 && memcmp(out, eb, 4) == 0);
    CHECK(rle_encode(a, sizeof a, out, 5) == 0);        /* too small */
    CHECK(rle_encode(a, 0, out, sizeof out) == 0);
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
Walk the input with an index `i`. Count how many bytes starting at `i` equal `in[i]`, stopping at 255. Emit the pair, then advance `i` by the count.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <string.h>

size_t rle_encode(const uint8_t *in, size_t n, uint8_t *out, size_t cap)
{
    size_t i = 0, o = 0;
    while (i < n) {
        uint8_t v = in[i];
        size_t run = 1;
        while (i + run < n && in[i + run] == v && run < 255) run++;
        if (o + 2 > cap) return 0;
        out[o++] = (uint8_t)run;
        out[o++] = v;
        i += run;
    }
    return o;
}
```
## Explanation
Plain RLE can double the size of data with no repeats, so real formats use a flag bit to mix literal runs and repeated runs (PackBits, the TGA format). The bounds check before each write is the important part: compression code that trusts its output buffer size is a common source of overflows.

**Follow-up:** write the decoder, checking that the expanded output fits its buffer before writing each run.

**Walk-through:** input `AA AA AA BB CC CC` → output pairs `(3, AA) (1, BB) (2, CC)` = 6 bytes.

**Splitting long runs:** a run of 300 identical bytes becomes `(255, X) (45, X)` because the count is one byte.

**Worst case:** input with no repeats doubles in size (every byte becomes a `(1, value)` pair), so `cap` must be checked before **every** write — returning 0 when the output does not fit, as the tests require.

**Decoder (for the follow-up)**

```c
size_t rle_decode(const uint8_t *in, size_t n, uint8_t *out, size_t cap)
{
    size_t w = 0;
    for (size_t i = 0; i + 1 < n; i += 2) {
        if (in[i] > cap - w) return 0;           /* would overflow out */
        for (uint8_t k = 0; k < in[i]; k++) out[w++] = in[i + 1];
    }
    return w;
}
```

**Better variants used in practice:** PackBits (a control byte says "next N literal bytes" or "repeat next byte N times", so non-repeating data grows by at most 1/128), bit-level RLE for 1-bit display bitmaps and fonts, and LZ-family compressors (heatshrink, LZ4) when RAM allows.

**Complexity:** O(n) time, O(1) extra memory — suitable for MCUs that decompress images straight into a display buffer.


@@@ two-sum-sorted-array
title: Two Sum in a Sorted Array (Two Pointers)
difficulty: E
category: Arrays & Searching
order: 25
## Problem
Given an array sorted in ascending order and a `target`, find two **different** elements whose sum equals `target`.

Implement `bool two_sum_sorted(const int *a, size_t n, int target, size_t *i, size_t *j)`. On success write the two indexes (`*i < *j`) and return `true`; return `false` if no pair exists. Use O(1) extra memory and a single pass.

Example: `{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}`, target `8` → indexes `1` and `5` (values 2 + 6). Any valid pair is accepted.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdbool.h>
#include <stddef.h>

bool two_sum_sorted(const int *a, size_t n, int target, size_t *i, size_t *j)
{
    return false;
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
static int ok(const int *a, size_t n, int t)
{
    size_t i = 99, j = 99;
    if (!two_sum_sorted(a, n, t, &i, &j)) return 0;
    return i < j && j < n && a[i] + a[j] == t;
}
int main(void)
{
    const int a[] = { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 };
    CHECK(ok(a, 6, 8));
    CHECK(ok(a, 6, 3));                       /* 1 + 2 */
    CHECK(ok(a, 6, 11));                      /* 5 + 6 */
    size_t i, j;
    CHECK(!two_sum_sorted(a, 6, 100, &i, &j));
    CHECK(!two_sum_sorted(a, 6, 2, &i, &j));  /* 1 + 1 not allowed: same element */
    const int d[] = { -5, -1, 0, 3, 3, 9 };
    CHECK(ok(d, 6, 6));                       /* 3 + 3, two different elements */
    CHECK(ok(d, 6, -6));
    CHECK(!two_sum_sorted(a, 0, 0, &i, &j));
    CHECK(!two_sum_sorted(a, 1, 2, &i, &j));
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
Start one pointer at each end. If the sum is too small, the only way to grow it is to move the **left** pointer right; if it is too large, move the **right** pointer left.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdbool.h>
#include <stddef.h>

bool two_sum_sorted(const int *a, size_t n, int target, size_t *i, size_t *j)
{
    if (n < 2) return false;
    size_t left = 0, right = n - 1;

    while (left < right) {
        long sum = (long)a[left] + a[right];   /* avoid int overflow */
        if (sum == target) {
            *i = left;
            *j = right;
            return true;
        }
        if (sum < target)
            left++;            /* need a bigger sum */
        else
            right--;           /* need a smaller sum */
    }
    return false;
}
```
## Explanation
Because the array is sorted, each comparison throws away one candidate for good:

- If `a[left] + a[right] < target`, then `a[left]` plus *any* element is too small (`a[right]` is already the largest left), so `left` can never be part of the answer.
- If the sum is too large, `a[right]` plus any element is too large, so drop `right`.

Each step moves one pointer, so it is **O(n) time, O(1) memory**, versus O(n²) for checking every pair.

**Walk-through: `{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}`, target 8**

| left | right | a[left] + a[right] | action |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | 5 | 1 + 6 = 7 | too small → left++ |
| 1 | 5 | 2 + 6 = 8 | found |

**Edge cases interviewers check:** fewer than 2 elements; no pair; the same element must not be used twice (`left < right` guarantees that); duplicates (`3 + 3`); negative numbers; overflow of `a[left] + a[right]` for large values, which is why the sum is computed as `long`.

**Follow-ups:** for an **unsorted** array, use a hash set for O(n) time and O(n) memory, or sort first for O(n log n). The same two-pointer idea solves three-sum (fix one element, two-pointer the rest) and "pair with the closest sum".

@@@ remove-duplicates-sorted
title: Remove Duplicates from a Sorted Array (In Place)
difficulty: E
category: Arrays & Searching
order: 26
## Problem
Given an array sorted in ascending order, remove the duplicates **in place** so each value appears once, keeping the original order. Return the new length. Elements beyond the new length don't matter. Use O(1) extra memory.

Example: `{1, 3, 3, 5, 5, 6, 9}` → length `5`, array starts `{1, 3, 5, 6, 9}`.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stddef.h>
#include <string.h>

size_t remove_duplicates_sorted(int *a, size_t n)
{
    return n;
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    int a[] = { 1, 3, 3, 5, 5, 6, 9 };
    const int ea[] = { 1, 3, 5, 6, 9 };
    CHECK(remove_duplicates_sorted(a, 7) == 5 && memcmp(a, ea, sizeof ea) == 0);
    int b[] = { 2, 2, 2, 2 };
    CHECK(remove_duplicates_sorted(b, 4) == 1 && b[0] == 2);
    int c[] = { 1, 2, 3 };
    CHECK(remove_duplicates_sorted(c, 3) == 3 && c[2] == 3);
    int d[] = { -3, -3, 0, 0, 0, 7 };
    const int ed[] = { -3, 0, 7 };
    CHECK(remove_duplicates_sorted(d, 6) == 3 && memcmp(d, ed, sizeof ed) == 0);
    CHECK(remove_duplicates_sorted(NULL, 0) == 0);
    int e[] = { 42 };
    CHECK(remove_duplicates_sorted(e, 1) == 1);
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
Duplicates are always next to each other in a sorted array. Keep a "write" index for the last unique value, and copy an element forward only when it differs from that value.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stddef.h>
#include <string.h>

size_t remove_duplicates_sorted(int *a, size_t n)
{
    if (n == 0) return 0;

    size_t w = 0;                    /* index of the last unique value */
    for (size_t r = 1; r < n; r++) {
        if (a[r] != a[w])
            a[++w] = a[r];           /* new value: keep it */
    }
    return w + 1;
}
```
## Explanation
This is the **read/write two-pointer** pattern: `r` scans every element, `w` marks the end of the unique prefix. Since equal values are adjacent, comparing with `a[w]` alone is enough. **O(n) time, O(1) memory**, one pass, and the relative order is preserved.

**Walk-through: `{1, 3, 3, 5, 5, 6, 9}`**

| r | a[r] | a[w] | action | unique prefix |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | 3 | 1 | copy to a[1] | 1 3 |
| 2 | 3 | 3 | skip | 1 3 |
| 3 | 5 | 3 | copy to a[2] | 1 3 5 |
| 4 | 5 | 5 | skip | 1 3 5 |
| 5 | 6 | 5 | copy to a[3] | 1 3 5 6 |
| 6 | 9 | 6 | copy to a[4] | 1 3 5 6 9 |

Return `w + 1 = 5`.

**Edge cases:** empty array (the classic bug is `n - 1` underflowing when `n` is unsigned), one element, all equal, no duplicates.

**Follow-ups:** allow each value at most twice (compare with `a[w - 1]`); remove all copies of a given value; for an **unsorted** array, see *Remove Duplicates Using a Hash Table*.

@@@ remove-duplicates-hash
title: Remove Duplicates from an Unsorted Array (Hash Table)
difficulty: M
category: Arrays & Searching
order: 27
## Problem
Remove duplicates from an **unsorted** array in place, keeping the **first** occurrence of each value and the original order. Return the new length, or `-1` if memory allocation fails.

Use a hash table so the whole operation runs in O(n) average time. Values can be any `int`, including negative numbers.

Example: `{5, 3, 1, 3, 9, 5, 6}` → length `5`, array starts `{5, 3, 1, 9, 6}`.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

int remove_duplicates_hash(int *a, int n)
{
    return n;
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    int a[] = { 5, 3, 1, 3, 9, 5, 6 };
    const int ea[] = { 5, 3, 1, 9, 6 };
    CHECK(remove_duplicates_hash(a, 7) == 5 && memcmp(a, ea, sizeof ea) == 0);
    int b[] = { -1, 7, -1, -8, 7, 0, -8 };
    const int eb[] = { -1, 7, -8, 0 };
    CHECK(remove_duplicates_hash(b, 7) == 4 && memcmp(b, eb, sizeof eb) == 0);
    int c[] = { 4, 4, 4 };
    CHECK(remove_duplicates_hash(c, 3) == 1 && c[0] == 4);
    int d[] = { -2147483647 - 1, 2147483647, -2147483647 - 1, 0 };
    CHECK(remove_duplicates_hash(d, 4) == 3);
    CHECK(remove_duplicates_hash(NULL, 0) == 0);
    int big[1000];
    for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) big[i] = (i * 7919) % 100;   /* only 100 distinct values */
    CHECK(remove_duplicates_hash(big, 1000) == 100 && big[0] == 0 && big[1] == 19);
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
Allocate an open-addressing table with about twice as many slots as elements, each slot holding a value and a "used" flag. For each element, hash it, then probe linearly until you find either the same value (a duplicate: skip it) or an empty slot (new: insert it and keep the element).
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

typedef struct {
    int value;
    unsigned char used;
} slot_t;

int remove_duplicates_hash(int *a, int n)
{
    if (n <= 0) return 0;

    size_t cap = 2 * (size_t)n + 1;              /* load factor < 0.5 */
    slot_t *tab = calloc(cap, sizeof *tab);
    if (!tab) return -1;

    int w = 0;
    for (int r = 0; r < n; r++) {
        size_t k = (unsigned int)a[r] % cap;     /* negatives handled by the cast */
        while (tab[k].used && tab[k].value != a[r])
            k = (k + 1) % cap;                   /* linear probing */

        if (!tab[k].used) {                      /* first time we see this value */
            tab[k].used = 1;
            tab[k].value = a[r];
            a[w++] = a[r];
        }
    }
    free(tab);
    return w;
}
```
## Explanation
The sorted-array trick does not work here because duplicates can be anywhere. A hash table remembers every value seen so far, so each lookup is O(1) on average and the whole pass is **O(n) average time, O(n) memory**. Writing kept elements through `w` preserves the order of first occurrence.

Design choices worth explaining in the interview:

- **Open addressing with linear probing:** one `calloc`, no per-node allocation, cache friendly.
- **Capacity `2n + 1`:** keeps the load factor below 50%, so probe sequences stay short and an empty slot always exists (the probe loop always terminates).
- **`(unsigned int)a[r] % cap`:** `%` on a negative `int` gives a negative result in C, which would index outside the table. Casting to unsigned first maps every value to a valid slot.
- **The `used` flag:** you cannot use 0 as "empty", because 0 is a valid value.
- **Check `calloc`** and free the table on every path.

**Walk-through: `{5, 3, 1, 3, 9, 5, 6}`, cap = 15**

| r | value | slot | result |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | 5 | 5 empty | keep → `5` |
| 1 | 3 | 3 empty | keep → `5 3` |
| 2 | 1 | 1 empty | keep → `5 3 1` |
| 3 | 3 | 3 holds 3 | duplicate, skip |
| 4 | 9 | 9 empty | keep → `5 3 1 9` |
| 5 | 5 | 5 holds 5 | duplicate, skip |
| 6 | 6 | 6 empty | keep → `5 3 1 9 6` |

**Trade-offs:** if you may reorder, sorting then using the sorted-array method is O(n log n) with O(1) extra memory, which is often preferred on a microcontroller with no heap. If the value range is small and known (e.g. 8-bit sensor readings), a 256-bit bitmap replaces the hash table entirely.

@@@ sliding-window-max-sum
title: Maximum Sum of k Consecutive Elements (Sliding Window)
difficulty: E
category: Arrays & Searching
order: 28
## Problem
Implement `bool max_window_sum(const int *a, size_t n, size_t k, long *out)` that finds the largest sum of any `k` **consecutive** elements and writes it to `*out`. Return `false` if `k` is 0 or larger than `n`. Run in O(n) time.

Example: `{2, 1, 5, 1, 3, 2}`, `k = 3` → `9` (5 + 1 + 3).

This is the same idea as a moving-average filter on sensor samples.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdbool.h>
#include <stddef.h>

bool max_window_sum(const int *a, size_t n, size_t k, long *out)
{
    return false;
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    long s = 0;
    const int a[] = { 2, 1, 5, 1, 3, 2 };
    CHECK(max_window_sum(a, 6, 3, &s) && s == 9);
    CHECK(max_window_sum(a, 6, 1, &s) && s == 5);
    CHECK(max_window_sum(a, 6, 6, &s) && s == 14);
    const int neg[] = { -4, -2, -7, -1 };
    CHECK(max_window_sum(neg, 4, 2, &s) && s == -6);      /* all negative */
    const int big[] = { 2000000000, 2000000000, 1 };
    CHECK(max_window_sum(big, 3, 2, &s) && s == 4000000000L);
    CHECK(!max_window_sum(a, 6, 0, &s));
    CHECK(!max_window_sum(a, 6, 7, &s));
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
Compute the sum of the first `k` elements once. Each time the window moves right by one, **add** the element entering and **subtract** the element leaving. Do not start `max` at 0: the array may be all negative.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdbool.h>
#include <stddef.h>

bool max_window_sum(const int *a, size_t n, size_t k, long *out)
{
    if (k == 0 || k > n) return false;

    long long sum = 0;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < k; i++)        /* first window */
        sum += a[i];

    long long best = sum;
    for (size_t i = k; i < n; i++) {
        sum += a[i] - (long long)a[i - k];  /* slide: add new, drop old */
        if (sum > best) best = sum;
    }
    *out = (long)best;
    return true;
}
```
## Explanation
Re-adding all `k` elements for every window costs **O(n·k)**. The sliding window reuses the previous sum, so each step is two additions: **O(n) time, O(1) memory**.

**Walk-through: `{2, 1, 5, 1, 3, 2}`, k = 3**

| window | operation | sum | best |
|---|---|---|---|
| [2 1 5] | initial | 8 | 8 |
| [1 5 1] | +1 −2 | 7 | 8 |
| [5 1 3] | +3 −1 | 9 | 9 |
| [1 3 2] | +2 −5 | 6 | 9 |

**Edge cases:** `k == 0` or `k > n` (the original version silently returned 0, which is also a valid sum, so a separate status is clearer); all-negative input (initialise `best` from the first window, never 0); overflow (two large `int`s overflow `int`, so accumulate in a 64-bit type).

**Follow-ups:** the **variable-size** window (shortest subarray with sum ≥ S, longest substring without repeating characters) grows the right edge and shrinks the left edge; the **maximum of each window** uses a deque for O(n). In firmware the same structure is a moving-average filter over a ring buffer.

@@@ binary-search
title: Binary Search in a Sorted Array
difficulty: E
category: Arrays & Searching
order: 29
## Problem
Implement `int binary_search(const int *a, int n, int target)` that returns the index of `target` in an array sorted ascending, or `-1` if it is not present. Use O(log n) comparisons and no recursion.

Example: `{2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14}`, target `12` → `5`.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>

int binary_search(const int *a, int n, int target)
{
    return -1;
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    const int a[] = { 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14 };
    CHECK(binary_search(a, 7, 12) == 5);
    CHECK(binary_search(a, 7, 2) == 0);           /* first */
    CHECK(binary_search(a, 7, 14) == 6);          /* last */
    CHECK(binary_search(a, 7, 1) == -1);          /* below range */
    CHECK(binary_search(a, 7, 15) == -1);         /* above range */
    CHECK(binary_search(a, 7, 7) == -1);          /* gap */
    CHECK(binary_search(a, 0, 2) == -1);          /* empty */
    const int one[] = { 5 };
    CHECK(binary_search(one, 1, 5) == 0);
    for (int i = 0; i < 7; i++) CHECK(binary_search(a, 7, a[i]) == i);
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
Keep an inclusive range `[low, high]`. Compare the target with the middle element and discard the half that cannot contain it. Compute the middle without overflowing.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>

int binary_search(const int *a, int n, int target)
{
    int low = 0, high = n - 1;              /* inclusive range */

    while (low <= high) {
        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;   /* no overflow */
        if (a[mid] == target)
            return mid;
        if (target < a[mid])
            high = mid - 1;                 /* search left half */
        else
            low = mid + 1;                  /* search right half */
    }
    return -1;
}
```
## Explanation
Each comparison halves the search range, so at most ⌈log₂(n+1)⌉ comparisons are needed: 20 for a million elements. **O(log n) time, O(1) memory.** The array **must** be sorted.

**Walk-through: `{2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14}`, target 12**

| low | high | mid | a[mid] | action |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | 6 | 3 | 8 | 12 > 8 → low = 4 |
| 4 | 6 | 5 | 12 | found at 5 |

**The bugs interviewers look for:**

- `mid = (low + high) / 2` can overflow when indexes are large; `low + (high - low) / 2` cannot.
- Mixing inclusive and exclusive bounds: with `high = n - 1` the loop is `low <= high` and the update is `high = mid - 1`. With `high = n` it is `low < high` and `high = mid`. Mixing them causes infinite loops or missed elements.
- Hard-coding the size (`high = 6`) instead of using `n`.

**Follow-ups:** find the **first or last** occurrence of a duplicated value (keep searching after a match); the **insertion point** (lower bound); search a **rotated** sorted array; binary search on the answer (e.g. the smallest baud-rate divisor that meets an error limit). In firmware, binary search is used on calibration and lookup tables in flash; see *Calibration Table Interpolation*.

@@@ kadane-max-subarray
title: Maximum Subarray Sum (Kadane's Algorithm)
difficulty: M
category: Arrays & Searching
order: 30
## Problem
Implement `long max_subarray_sum(const int *a, size_t n)` that returns the largest sum of any **non-empty contiguous** subarray. Run in O(n) time and O(1) memory. Assume `n >= 1`.

Example: `{-2, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4}` → `6` (subarray `4, -1, 2, 1`).
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stddef.h>

long max_subarray_sum(const int *a, size_t n)
{
    return 0;
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    const int a[] = { -2, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4 };
    CHECK(max_subarray_sum(a, 9) == 6);
    const int neg[] = { -8, -3, -6, -2, -5 };
    CHECK(max_subarray_sum(neg, 5) == -2);          /* all negative: best single element */
    const int pos[] = { 1, 2, 3 };
    CHECK(max_subarray_sum(pos, 3) == 6);           /* whole array */
    const int one[] = { -7 };
    CHECK(max_subarray_sum(one, 1) == -7);
    const int mix[] = { 5, -9, 6, -2, 3 };
    CHECK(max_subarray_sum(mix, 5) == 7);           /* 6 - 2 + 3 */
    const int big[] = { 2000000000, 2000000000 };
    CHECK(max_subarray_sum(big, 2) == 4000000000L);
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
For each position, the best subarray **ending here** either extends the best one ending at the previous position, or starts fresh at this element, whichever is larger. Track the overall best as you go.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stddef.h>

long max_subarray_sum(const int *a, size_t n)
{
    long long cur = a[0];     /* best sum of a subarray ending at i */
    long long best = a[0];    /* best sum seen anywhere */

    for (size_t i = 1; i < n; i++) {
        long long extend = cur + a[i];
        cur = (extend > a[i]) ? extend : a[i];   /* extend or restart */
        if (cur > best) best = cur;
    }
    return (long)best;
}
```
## Explanation
Kadane's algorithm is dynamic programming with one variable of state. If the running sum `cur` becomes negative, carrying it forward can only make later sums smaller, so starting fresh at `a[i]` is better. That single decision per element gives **O(n) time, O(1) memory**, versus O(n²) for checking every subarray.

**Walk-through: `{-2, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4}`**

| i | a[i] | cur = max(cur + a[i], a[i]) | best |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | −2 | −2 | −2 |
| 1 | 1 | max(−1, 1) = 1 | 1 |
| 2 | −3 | max(−2, −3) = −2 | 1 |
| 3 | 4 | max(2, 4) = 4 | 4 |
| 4 | −1 | 3 | 4 |
| 5 | 2 | 5 | 5 |
| 6 | 1 | 6 | **6** |
| 7 | −5 | 1 | 6 |
| 8 | 4 | 5 | 6 |

**Edge cases:** an all-negative array must return the largest single element, not 0, which is why both variables start at `a[0]` instead of 0; overflow, handled with a 64-bit accumulator.

**Follow-ups:** return the start and end indexes (record `i` when restarting and when `best` improves); maximum **circular** subarray (max of Kadane and total − minimum subarray); maximum **product** subarray (track both max and min because a negative flips them).

@@@ dutch-national-flag
title: Sort 0s, 1s and 2s in One Pass (Dutch National Flag)
difficulty: M
category: Arrays & Searching
order: 31
## Problem
An array contains only the values `0`, `1` and `2`. Sort it **in place in a single pass** with O(1) extra memory and without counting.

Implement `void sort_012(int *a, size_t n)`.

Example: `{2, 0, 2, 1, 1, 0}` → `{0, 0, 1, 1, 2, 2}`.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stddef.h>
#include <string.h>

void sort_012(int *a, size_t n)
{
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    int a[] = { 2, 0, 2, 1, 1, 0 };
    const int ea[] = { 0, 0, 1, 1, 2, 2 };
    sort_012(a, 6); CHECK(memcmp(a, ea, sizeof ea) == 0);
    int b[] = { 2, 2, 2 };
    sort_012(b, 3); CHECK(b[0] == 2 && b[2] == 2);
    int c[] = { 2, 1, 0 };
    const int ec[] = { 0, 1, 2 };
    sort_012(c, 3); CHECK(memcmp(c, ec, sizeof ec) == 0);
    int d[] = { 0 };
    sort_012(d, 1); CHECK(d[0] == 0);
    sort_012(NULL, 0);
    int e[] = { 1, 2, 0, 2, 0, 1, 1, 0, 2, 0 };
    const int ee[] = { 0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 2 };
    sort_012(e, 10); CHECK(memcmp(e, ee, sizeof ee) == 0);
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
Use three indexes: everything before `low` is 0, everything after `high` is 2, and `mid` scans the unknown middle. A 0 is swapped to `low`, a 2 is swapped to `high`, a 1 stays. After swapping with `high`, do **not** advance `mid`: the value you swapped in is still unchecked.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stddef.h>
#include <string.h>

static void swap(int *x, int *y) { int t = *x; *x = *y; *y = t; }

void sort_012(int *a, size_t n)
{
    if (n == 0) return;
    size_t low = 0, mid = 0, high = n - 1;

    while (mid <= high) {
        if (a[mid] == 0) {
            swap(&a[low++], &a[mid++]);
        } else if (a[mid] == 1) {
            mid++;
        } else {                       /* a[mid] == 2 */
            swap(&a[mid], &a[high]);
            if (high == 0) break;      /* avoid size_t underflow */
            high--;                    /* mid stays: new a[mid] is unchecked */
        }
    }
}
```
## Explanation
Dijkstra's Dutch National Flag algorithm keeps four regions at all times:

```
[0 .. low-1]   all 0s
[low .. mid-1] all 1s
[mid .. high]  not yet examined
[high+1 .. n-1] all 2s
```

Each step shrinks the unknown region by one, so it is **one pass, O(n) time, O(1) memory**.

**Walk-through: `{2, 0, 2, 1, 1, 0}`**

| low | mid | high | a[mid] | action | array |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | 0 | 5 | 2 | swap mid↔high, high-- | 0 0 2 1 1 2 |
| 0 | 0 | 4 | 0 | swap low↔mid, low++, mid++ | 0 0 2 1 1 2 |
| 1 | 1 | 4 | 0 | swap low↔mid, low++, mid++ | 0 0 2 1 1 2 |
| 2 | 2 | 4 | 2 | swap mid↔high, high-- | 0 0 1 1 2 2 |
| 2 | 2 | 3 | 1 | mid++ | 0 0 1 1 2 2 |
| 2 | 3 | 3 | 1 | mid++ → done | 0 0 1 1 2 2 |

**Why `mid` does not move after the 2-swap:** the element brought in from `high` has not been examined yet; it could be a 0 that must still go left. After a 0-swap, the element coming from `low` is already known to be a 1 (or it is the same element), so `mid` can advance.

**Watch out in C:** with unsigned `size_t` indexes, `high--` when `high == 0` wraps to a huge number, so the solution stops explicitly. The original `int` version avoids this but cannot handle arrays larger than `INT_MAX`.

**Follow-ups:** counting sort (two passes) is simpler if a single pass is not required; the same partitioning is the core of three-way quicksort, which handles many duplicate keys efficiently; "move all zeros to the end" and "segregate even/odd" are two-region versions.

@@@ single-number-xor
title: Find the Element That Appears Once (XOR)
difficulty: E
category: Bit Manipulation
order: 32
## Problem
Every element of an array appears **exactly twice**, except one element that appears once. Find that element in O(n) time and O(1) extra memory, without sorting.

Implement `int single_number(const int *a, size_t n)`.

Example: `{4, 1, 2, 1, 2}` → `4`.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stddef.h>

int single_number(const int *a, size_t n)
{
    return 0;
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    const int a[] = { 4, 1, 2, 1, 2 };
    CHECK(single_number(a, 5) == 4);
    const int b[] = { 7 };
    CHECK(single_number(b, 1) == 7);
    const int c[] = { -3, 9, 9, 0, 0 };
    CHECK(single_number(c, 5) == -3);
    const int d[] = { 2, 2, 0 };
    CHECK(single_number(d, 3) == 0);
    const int e[] = { 2147483647, 5, -2147483647 - 1, 5, 2147483647 };
    CHECK(single_number(e, 5) == -2147483647 - 1);
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
What is `x ^ x`? What is `x ^ 0`? XOR is also commutative and associative, so the order of the elements doesn't matter.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stddef.h>

int single_number(const int *a, size_t n)
{
    unsigned int r = 0;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++)
        r ^= (unsigned int)a[i];     /* pairs cancel out */
    return (int)r;
}
```
## Explanation
Three properties of XOR do all the work:

- `x ^ x = 0`: every value that appears twice cancels itself.
- `x ^ 0 = x`: the value that appears once survives.
- XOR is commutative and associative: the pairs don't need to be next to each other.

So XOR-ing the whole array leaves exactly the unpaired element. **O(n) time, O(1) memory**, no sorting and no hash table.

**Walk-through: `{4, 1, 2, 1, 2}`** (binary)

| step | value | r |
|---|---|---|
| start | | 000 |
| ^ 4 | 100 | 100 |
| ^ 1 | 001 | 101 |
| ^ 2 | 010 | 111 |
| ^ 1 | 001 | 110 |
| ^ 2 | 010 | 100 = **4** |

Doing the XOR on `unsigned int` keeps every bit pattern well defined, including negative numbers.

**Follow-ups interviewers ask next:**

- **Two** elements appear once: XOR everything to get `x ^ y`, take any set bit (`v & -v`) to split the array into two groups, and XOR each group.
- Every other element appears **three** times: count each bit position modulo 3.
- Find the **missing number** in `0..n`: XOR all indexes and all values together.
- Same trick in firmware: XOR checksums in serial protocols (see *UART Packet Parser*) and RAID-style parity.

@@@ implement-memcpy
title: Implement memcpy
difficulty: E
category: Pointers & Strings
order: 33
## Problem
Implement `void *my_memcpy(void *dst, const void *src, size_t n)` that copies exactly `n` bytes from `src` to `dst` and returns `dst`. The regions do not overlap. Do not use any library function.

This is one of the most common embedded C interview questions. Be ready to explain why the pointers are cast to `unsigned char *` and how it differs from `memmove`.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stddef.h>
#include <string.h>

void *my_memcpy(void *dst, const void *src, size_t n)
{
    return dst;
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    char src[] = "embedded";
    char dst[16] = { 0 };
    CHECK(my_memcpy(dst, src, 9) == dst && strcmp(dst, "embedded") == 0);

    unsigned char raw[5] = { 0x00, 0xFF, 0x80, 0x7F, 0x00 };   /* zeros inside: not a string */
    unsigned char out[5] = { 1, 1, 1, 1, 1 };
    my_memcpy(out, raw, 5);
    CHECK(memcmp(out, raw, 5) == 0);

    int a[4] = { 1, -2, 300000, -400000 }, b[4] = { 0 };
    my_memcpy(b, a, sizeof a);
    CHECK(memcmp(a, b, sizeof a) == 0);

    char g[4] = { 'x', 'x', 'x', 'x' };
    CHECK(my_memcpy(g, "ab", 0) == g && g[0] == 'x');            /* n = 0 copies nothing */
    my_memcpy(g + 1, "ab", 2);
    CHECK(g[0] == 'x' && g[1] == 'a' && g[2] == 'b' && g[3] == 'x'); /* no overrun */
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
`void *` cannot be dereferenced or incremented in standard C. Convert both pointers to byte pointers (`unsigned char *`), copy one byte per iteration, and remember to return the **original** `dst`.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stddef.h>
#include <string.h>

void *my_memcpy(void *dst, const void *src, size_t n)
{
    unsigned char *d = dst;            /* void * converts implicitly in C */
    const unsigned char *s = src;

    while (n--)
        *d++ = *s++;

    return dst;                        /* original pointer, not d */
}
```
## Explanation
Your version is correct. The casts `(unsigned char *)dst` are optional in C, because `void *` converts to any object pointer implicitly (they are required in C++). `while (n--)` is the same loop written shorter.

**Why `unsigned char`?** The standard guarantees that any object can be accessed byte by byte through a character type, which is exempt from strict aliasing. `unsigned char` has no sign or padding problems, so every bit pattern copies exactly.

**Walk-through: copy 3 bytes `"abc"`**

| n before | copy | d moves to |
|---|---|---|
| 3 | `d[0] = 'a'` | d + 1 |
| 2 | `d[1] = 'b'` | d + 2 |
| 1 | `d[2] = 'c'` | d + 3 |
| 0 | loop ends, return original `dst` | |

**memcpy vs memmove:** `memcpy` assumes the buffers do **not** overlap; its parameters are declared `restrict`, and overlapping calls are undefined behaviour. `memmove` handles overlap by copying backwards when needed (see *Implement memmove*).

**Follow-ups interviewers ask:**
- *How would you make it faster?* Copy bytes until `dst` is word-aligned, then copy 4 or 8 bytes at a time, then the remaining tail. Only do word copies if `src` is aligned too, or the CPU supports unaligned access (Cortex-M0 faults on it). Real libraries also use SIMD or DMA.
- *Can the compiler replace your loop?* Yes, GCC may turn the loop into a call to `memcpy` itself, which recurses forever if you are *writing* `memcpy`. Build library code with `-fno-builtin` or `-ffreestanding`.
- *What about `volatile` memory such as device registers or DMA buffers?* `memcpy` may merge or reorder accesses, so copy register blocks with an explicit loop over `volatile` pointers.

@@@ implement-strlen
title: Implement strlen
difficulty: E
category: Pointers & Strings
order: 34
## Problem
Implement `size_t my_strlen(const char *s)` that returns the number of characters before the terminating `'\0'`, without using the standard library.

Example: `my_strlen("hello")` → `5`, `my_strlen("")` → `0`.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stddef.h>

size_t my_strlen(const char *s)
{
    return 0;
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    CHECK(my_strlen("hello") == 5);
    CHECK(my_strlen("") == 0);
    CHECK(my_strlen("a") == 1);
    CHECK(my_strlen("embedded interview") == 18);
    char buf[] = { 'a', 'b', '\0', 'c', '\0' };
    CHECK(my_strlen(buf) == 2);                 /* stops at the first NUL */
    const char *s = "\xFF\x80 high bytes";
    CHECK(my_strlen(s) == 13);
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
Walk the string until you reach `'\0'`. Count with a `size_t`, not an `int`. You can count with an index or by subtracting two pointers.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stddef.h>

size_t my_strlen(const char *s)
{
    const char *p = s;
    while (*p)
        p++;
    return (size_t)(p - s);      /* distance from start to the '\0' */
}
```
## Explanation
Your index version (`while (s[len] != '\0') len++;`) is correct. The pointer version above does the same thing: advance to the terminator and subtract the start address.

**Walk-through: `"abc"`**

| p points at | `*p` | action |
|---|---|---|
| s + 0 | 'a' | p++ |
| s + 1 | 'b' | p++ |
| s + 2 | 'c' | p++ |
| s + 3 | '\0' | stop, return 3 |

**Points interviewers check:**
- **Return type `size_t`**: lengths are never negative and can exceed `INT_MAX`. A common follow-up bug is `for (int i = 0; i < strlen(s) - 1; i++)`: for an empty string, `strlen(s) - 1` is a huge unsigned number.
- **O(n) every call**: calling `strlen` in a loop condition makes the loop O(n²). Compute it once.
- **`NULL`**: the standard `strlen(NULL)` is undefined; don't hide bugs by returning 0 silently.
- **No terminator**: on a buffer filled from a UART or network packet there may be no `'\0'`, and `strlen` reads past the end. Use a bounded version (`strnlen(s, maxlen)`) for untrusted data.

**Faster versions** check one word at a time once the pointer is aligned, using the zero-byte trick `(v - 0x01010101) & ~v & 0x80808080` from the Bit Manipulation topic.

@@@ implement-strcpy
title: Implement strcpy
difficulty: E
category: Pointers & Strings
order: 35
## Problem
Implement `char *my_strcpy(char *dst, const char *src)` that copies the string `src`, **including** the terminating `'\0'`, into `dst` and returns `dst`. Assume `dst` is large enough.

Then be ready to explain why `strcpy` is considered dangerous and what to use instead.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

char *my_strcpy(char *dst, const char *src)
{
    return dst;
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    char d[32];
    memset(d, 'X', sizeof d);
    CHECK(my_strcpy(d, "firmware") == d && strcmp(d, "firmware") == 0);
    CHECK(d[8] == '\0' && d[9] == 'X');           /* terminator copied, nothing after it */
    CHECK(my_strcpy(d, "") == d && d[0] == '\0');
    char e[8];
    CHECK(strcmp(my_strcpy(e, "abc"), "abc") == 0);   /* return value is usable */
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
Copy one character at a time and stop **after** you have copied the `'\0'`. Save the original `dst` first, because you will move the pointer.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

char *my_strcpy(char *dst, const char *src)
{
    char *ret = dst;
    while ((*dst++ = *src++) != '\0')
        ;                          /* copy includes the '\0' */
    return ret;
}
```
## Explanation
Your version is correct: the assignment inside the condition copies the character first, then tests it, so the terminator is copied before the loop stops. The shorter form above is the classic K&R idiom.

**Walk-through: `"hi"`**

| step | copied | value tested | continue? |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | dst[0] = 'h' | 'h' | yes |
| 2 | dst[1] = 'i' | 'i' | yes |
| 3 | dst[2] = '\0' | '\0' | no → return original dst |

**Why `strcpy` is dangerous:** it has no idea how big `dst` is. If `src` is longer, it writes past the end of the buffer, a **buffer overflow** that corrupts the stack or heap and is a classic security hole. MISRA C and CERT C forbid it with untrusted input.

**Safer alternatives:**
- `snprintf(dst, size, "%s", src)`: always terminates, returns the length it wanted to write.
- `strlcpy` (BSD, glibc 2.38+, POSIX 2024): always terminates, reports truncation (see *Safe String Copy*).
- **Not** `strncpy`: it does not terminate `dst` when `src` is too long, and it pads the rest with zeros.

**Other follow-ups:** overlapping `src` and `dst` are undefined behaviour; `strcpy` returns `dst` so calls can be chained, but that means you cannot easily get the end position (POSIX `stpcpy` returns a pointer to the new terminator, useful for appending).

@@@ implement-strcmp
title: Implement strcmp
difficulty: E
category: Pointers & Strings
order: 36
## Problem
Implement `int my_strcmp(const char *s1, const char *s2)` that returns a negative value if `s1` sorts before `s2`, zero if they are equal, and a positive value if `s1` sorts after `s2`. Characters must be compared as **unsigned char**, like the standard function.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>

int my_strcmp(const char *s1, const char *s2)
{
    return 0;
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    CHECK(my_strcmp("abc", "abc") == 0);
    CHECK(my_strcmp("abc", "abd") < 0);
    CHECK(my_strcmp("abd", "abc") > 0);
    CHECK(my_strcmp("ab", "abc") < 0);             /* prefix sorts first */
    CHECK(my_strcmp("abc", "ab") > 0);
    CHECK(my_strcmp("", "") == 0);
    CHECK(my_strcmp("", "a") < 0);
    CHECK(my_strcmp("\xE9", "a") > 0);             /* 0xE9 > 'a' when unsigned */
    CHECK(my_strcmp("A", "a") < 0);                /* case-sensitive: 'A' = 65 */
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
Advance while both characters are equal and not the terminator. Then return the difference of the first characters that differ, converted to `unsigned char`.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>

int my_strcmp(const char *s1, const char *s2)
{
    const unsigned char *a = (const unsigned char *)s1;
    const unsigned char *b = (const unsigned char *)s2;

    while (*a && *a == *b) {
        a++;
        b++;
    }
    return *a - *b;          /* both promote to int: no overflow */
}
```
## Explanation
Your version is correct, including the important `(unsigned char)` casts. Converting the pointers once, as above, is equivalent.

The loop stops at the first difference **or** at the end of `s1`. You don't need to check `*b` separately: if `s2` ended first, `*b` is `'\0'` and differs from `*a`, so the loop already stopped.

**Walk-through: `"ab"` vs `"abc"`**

| a | b | equal? | action |
|---|---|---|---|
| 'a' | 'a' | yes | advance |
| 'b' | 'b' | yes | advance |
| '\0' | 'c' | stop (end of s1) | return 0 − 99 = −99 (< 0) |

**Why the unsigned cast matters:** `char` is signed on x86 and on Arm with some compilers. Without the cast, a byte like `0xE9` ('é' in Latin-1) is −23 and would wrongly sort **before** 'a'. The C standard says `strcmp` compares as `unsigned char`.

**Points to mention:**
- Only the **sign** of the result is defined. Never write `if (strcmp(a, b) == -1)`.
- `a - b` cannot overflow, because both values are 0–255 promoted to `int`.
- Follow-ups: `strncmp` (stop after n characters, used for protocol prefixes like `"AT+"`), case-insensitive compare, and constant-time compare for passwords or MACs so the timing doesn't leak how many characters matched.

@@@ reverse-bits-32
title: Reverse the Bits of a 32-bit Integer
difficulty: E
category: Bit Manipulation
order: 37
## Problem
Implement `uint32_t reverse_bits(uint32_t n)` that mirrors all 32 bits: bit 0 becomes bit 31, bit 1 becomes bit 30, and so on.

Example: `0x0000000F` → `0xF0000000`; `0x00000001` → `0x80000000`.

Write the simple loop first, then the constant-time version interviewers ask for next.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

uint32_t reverse_bits(uint32_t n)
{
    return n;
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    CHECK(reverse_bits(0x0000000Fu) == 0xF0000000u);
    CHECK(reverse_bits(0x00000001u) == 0x80000000u);
    CHECK(reverse_bits(0x80000000u) == 0x00000001u);
    CHECK(reverse_bits(0x00000000u) == 0x00000000u);
    CHECK(reverse_bits(0xFFFFFFFFu) == 0xFFFFFFFFu);
    CHECK(reverse_bits(0x12345678u) == 0x1E6A2C48u);
    CHECK(reverse_bits(reverse_bits(0xDEADBEEFu)) == 0xDEADBEEFu);
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
Loop 32 times: shift the result left to make room, copy the lowest bit of `n` into it, then shift `n` right.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

/* Simple version: 32 iterations */
uint32_t reverse_bits(uint32_t n)
{
    uint32_t rev = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < 32; i++) {
        rev = (rev << 1) | (n & 1u);   /* append lowest bit of n */
        n >>= 1;
    }
    return rev;
}

/* Constant-time version: swap ever-larger groups (also correct) */
uint32_t reverse_bits_fast(uint32_t n)
{
    n = ((n & 0x55555555u) << 1) | ((n >> 1) & 0x55555555u);  /* swap single bits */
    n = ((n & 0x33333333u) << 2) | ((n >> 2) & 0x33333333u);  /* swap pairs       */
    n = ((n & 0x0F0F0F0Fu) << 4) | ((n >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0Fu);  /* swap nibbles     */
    n = ((n & 0x00FF00FFu) << 8) | ((n >> 8) & 0x00FF00FFu);  /* swap bytes       */
    return (n << 16) | (n >> 16);                              /* swap halves      */
}
```
## Explanation
Both of your versions are correct.

**Loop version:** each iteration takes the lowest bit off `n` and pushes it onto the right of `rev`. After 32 steps, the bit that started at position 0 has been shifted left 31 times, so it ends at position 31. It must run all 32 iterations even when `n` becomes 0 early, otherwise leading bits are lost.

**Walk-through on 4 bits, n = 0b0011**

| i | n & 1 | rev | n after |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | 1 | 0001 | 0001 |
| 1 | 1 | 0011 | 0000 |
| 2 | 0 | 0110 | 0000 |
| 3 | 0 | 1100 | 0000 |

**Fast version:** five swap stages of width 1, 2, 4, 8 and 16 bits. Each stage uses a mask to select one half of every group, moves it by the group width and merges. That is about 15 operations with no loop or branch, which is O(log w) for a w-bit word.

**On real hardware:** Armv7-M and later have a single instruction, `RBIT` (`__RBIT()` in CMSIS); Clang also provides `__builtin_bitreverse32`. A 256-entry byte table is another fast option when flash is available.

**Small fixes worth mentioning:** use `1u` and `u`-suffixed masks so all arithmetic is unsigned, and print a `uint32_t` with `"%08" PRIX32` from `<inttypes.h>` rather than `%08X`, which assumes `unsigned int` is 32 bits.

**Where it is used:** LSB-first vs MSB-first peripherals, CRC implementations with reflected polynomials, and FFT bit-reversed addressing.

@@@ first-set-bit
title: Position of the First (Lowest) Set Bit
difficulty: E
category: Bit Manipulation
order: 38
## Problem
Implement `int first_set_bit(uint32_t n)` that returns the **1-based** position of the lowest set bit, or `0` if no bit is set. This matches the POSIX `ffs()` function.

Example: `18` (binary `10010`) → `2`; `1` → `1`; `0x80000000` → `32`; `0` → `0`.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

int first_set_bit(uint32_t n)
{
    return 0;
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    CHECK(first_set_bit(18) == 2);
    CHECK(first_set_bit(1) == 1);
    CHECK(first_set_bit(0) == 0);
    CHECK(first_set_bit(0x80000000u) == 32);
    CHECK(first_set_bit(0xFFFFFFFFu) == 1);
    CHECK(first_set_bit(0x00010000u) == 17);
    CHECK(first_set_bit(12) == 3);              /* 1100 */
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
Handle `0` first, or the loop never ends. Then shift right until the lowest bit is 1, counting the shifts. For a faster version, `n & -n` isolates the lowest set bit.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

int first_set_bit(uint32_t n)
{
    if (n == 0)
        return 0;              /* no set bit: also prevents an endless loop */

    int pos = 1;
    while ((n & 1u) == 0) {
        n >>= 1;
        pos++;
    }
    return pos;
}
```
## Explanation
Your version is correct, and the `n == 0` check is essential: without it the loop shifts zeros forever.

**Walk-through: n = 18 = 0b10010**

| n | n & 1 | pos |
|---|---|---|
| 10010 | 0 | 1 → shift |
| 01001 | 1 | 2 → stop |

Result: 2.

**Faster ways (what interviewers ask next):**
- `n & -n` (or `n & (~n + 1)`) keeps only the lowest set bit: 18 → `00010`. Its position is then a single count.
- Built-ins: `__builtin_ctz(n) + 1` (count trailing zeros, undefined for 0) or `__builtin_ffs(n)`, which compile to one or two instructions. On Arm, `ctz` is `RBIT` followed by `CLZ`.
- Highest set bit instead: `32 - __builtin_clz(n)` gives the 1-based position (this is ⌊log₂ n⌋ + 1).

**Where it is used in firmware:** finding the highest-priority pending interrupt or ready task from a bitmap (RTOS schedulers such as FreeRTOS's optimised port and Zephyr do this with `CLZ`), allocating the first free slot from a bitmap, and decoding which flag in a status register fired.

**Note the convention:** this question uses 1-based positions with 0 meaning "none". If an interviewer asks for 0-based positions, you need another way to signal "no bit set", such as returning −1.

@@@ check-endianness
title: Detect the CPU's Endianness
difficulty: E
category: Bit Manipulation
order: 39
## Problem
Write `int is_little_endian(void)` that returns `1` if the CPU stores multi-byte values little-endian and `0` if big-endian, by inspecting how a known value is laid out in memory.

The online compiler runs on x86-64, which is little-endian, so the test expects `1`.
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

int is_little_endian(void)
{
    return 0;
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    CHECK(is_little_endian() == 1);   /* x86-64 and almost all Arm systems */
    CHECK(is_little_endian() == is_little_endian());
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
Store a value whose bytes are all different, such as `0x12345678`, then look at the byte at the lowest address through an `unsigned char` pointer.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

int is_little_endian(void)
{
    uint32_t x = 0x12345678u;
    const unsigned char *p = (const unsigned char *)&x;
    return p[0] == 0x78;          /* lowest address holds the least significant byte */
}
```
## Explanation
Your program is correct. Accessing an object through `unsigned char *` is always allowed in C, so this is portable and has no strict-aliasing problem.

**How `0x12345678` is laid out**

| address | little-endian | big-endian |
|---|---|---|
| p[0] | 0x78 | 0x12 |
| p[1] | 0x56 | 0x34 |
| p[2] | 0x34 | 0x56 |
| p[3] | 0x12 | 0x78 |

**Other ways to answer:**
- A union: `union { uint32_t u; uint8_t b[4]; } v = { 0x01 }; return v.b[0];` (fine in C, not in C++).
- At compile time with GCC/Clang: `#if __BYTE_ORDER__ == __ORDER_LITTLE_ENDIAN__`. This is better in real firmware because it costs nothing at run time.

**The point interviewers want you to make:** you rarely need to *detect* endianness. When you parse a protocol or file format, build values from bytes with shifts, for example `(uint32_t)b[0] << 24 | b[1] << 16 | b[2] << 8 | b[3]` for big-endian data. That works on any CPU without checking anything (see *Swap Byte Order of a 32-bit Value*).

**Where it matters:** network byte order is big-endian (`htonl`/`ntohl`); CAN signals, Modbus registers and many sensors use big-endian fields; x86, RISC-V and almost all Arm systems run little-endian; some PowerPC and older networking chips are big-endian.

@@@ swap-odd-even-bits
title: Swap Odd and Even Bits
difficulty: E
category: Bit Manipulation
order: 40
## Problem
Implement `uint32_t swap_odd_even_bits(uint32_t n)` that swaps every pair of adjacent bits: bit 0 with bit 1, bit 2 with bit 3, and so on.

Example: `23` (binary `0001 0111`) → `43` (binary `0010 1011`).
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

uint32_t swap_odd_even_bits(uint32_t n)
{
    return n;
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
int main(void)
{
    CHECK(swap_odd_even_bits(23) == 43);
    CHECK(swap_odd_even_bits(1) == 2);
    CHECK(swap_odd_even_bits(2) == 1);
    CHECK(swap_odd_even_bits(0xAAAAAAAAu) == 0x55555555u);
    CHECK(swap_odd_even_bits(0x55555555u) == 0xAAAAAAAAu);
    CHECK(swap_odd_even_bits(0x80000000u) == 0x40000000u);
    CHECK(swap_odd_even_bits(0xFFFFFFFFu) == 0xFFFFFFFFu);
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
`0x55555555` has a 1 in every **even** position (0, 2, 4…) and `0xAAAAAAAA` in every **odd** position. Pick out each set, move it one place towards the other, and combine.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

uint32_t swap_odd_even_bits(uint32_t n)
{
    uint32_t even = n & 0x55555555u;   /* bits 0, 2, 4, ... */
    uint32_t odd  = n & 0xAAAAAAAAu;   /* bits 1, 3, 5, ... */
    return (even << 1) | (odd >> 1);
}
```
## Explanation
Your version is correct. Each even bit moves up one position into the odd slot next to it, and each odd bit moves down. Because the two masks never overlap, OR-ing the shifted halves cannot collide. It runs in a fixed handful of instructions, with no loop.

**Walk-through: n = 23 = 0b0001 0111**

| step | binary | value |
|---|---|---|
| n | 0001 0111 | 23 |
| even = n & 0x55 | 0001 0101 | 21 |
| odd = n & 0xAA | 0000 0010 | 2 |
| even << 1 | 0010 1010 | 42 |
| odd >> 1 | 0000 0001 | 1 |
| result | 0010 1011 | **43** |

**Why `0x55` and `0xAA`:** `0x5` is `0101` and `0xA` is `1010`, so repeating them gives the even-bit and odd-bit masks for any width.

**Points to mention:**
- Use **unsigned** types: with a signed `int`, `0xAAAAAAAA` does not fit and `>>` of a negative value is implementation-defined.
- This is exactly the **first stage** of the fast bit-reversal (see *Reverse the Bits of a 32-bit Integer*). Swapping pairs with `0x33333333`, nibbles with `0x0F0F0F0F` and bytes with `0x00FF00FF` are the other stages.
- **Follow-up:** swap nibbles in a byte, `(b << 4) | (b >> 4)`, which is used to convert BCD and some LCD controller data.

@@@ container-of
title: Implement container_of
difficulty: M
category: Pointers & Strings
order: 41
## Problem
Linux and most RTOS kernels embed list nodes **inside** the structures they link. Given a pointer to the embedded member, you need the address of the structure that contains it.

1. Implement the macro `container_of(ptr, type, member)`.
2. Use it in `struct sensor *sensor_from_node(struct list_node *n)`.

```c
struct list_node { struct list_node *next; };
struct sensor { int id; char name[12]; struct list_node node; float value; };
```
## Starter code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stddef.h>

#define container_of(ptr, type, member) ((type *)0)

struct list_node { struct list_node *next; };
struct sensor { int id; char name[12]; struct list_node node; float value; };

struct sensor *sensor_from_node(struct list_node *n)
{
    return NULL;
}

/* ---- tests ---- */
static int fails;
#define CHECK(c) do { if (!(c)) { printf("FAIL line %d: %s\n", __LINE__, #c); fails++; } } while (0)
struct pair { char tag; double d; int first; };
int main(void)
{
    struct sensor s[2] = { { 1, "temp", { NULL }, 21.5f }, { 2, "humidity", { NULL }, 40.0f } };
    s[0].node.next = &s[1].node;

    CHECK(sensor_from_node(&s[0].node) == &s[0]);
    CHECK(sensor_from_node(s[0].node.next) == &s[1]);
    CHECK(sensor_from_node(s[0].node.next)->id == 2);

    int sum = 0;                                    /* walk the list */
    for (struct list_node *p = &s[0].node; p; p = p->next)
        sum += sensor_from_node(p)->id;
    CHECK(sum == 3);

    struct pair pr;
    CHECK(container_of(&pr.first, struct pair, first) == &pr);  /* member not first, padding */
    CHECK(container_of(&pr.tag, struct pair, tag) == &pr);      /* member at offset 0 */
    puts(fails ? "Some tests failed" : "All tests passed");
    return fails != 0;
}
```
## Hint
`offsetof(type, member)` from `<stddef.h>` gives the member's byte offset inside the structure. Convert the member pointer to `char *` so the subtraction is in bytes, subtract the offset, and cast to `type *`.
## Solution
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stddef.h>

#define container_of(ptr, type, member) \
    ((type *)((char *)(ptr) - offsetof(type, member)))

struct list_node { struct list_node *next; };
struct sensor { int id; char name[12]; struct list_node node; float value; };

struct sensor *sensor_from_node(struct list_node *n)
{
    return n ? container_of(n, struct sensor, node) : NULL;
}
```
## Explanation
Your macro is correct and identical to the classic definition. It needs `#include <stddef.h>` for `offsetof`.

**How it works:** a member lives at a fixed byte offset from the start of its structure. If `&s.node` is address `A` and `offsetof(struct sensor, node)` is 16, the structure starts at `A − 16`.

**Example layout on a 64-bit machine**

| member | offset | size |
|---|---|---|
| `id` | 0 | 4 |
| `name[12]` | 4 | 12 |
| `node` | 16 | 8 |
| `value` | 24 | 4 |

Given `ptr = 0x1010` for `node`, the macro computes `0x1010 − 16 = 0x1000`, the address of the `struct sensor`.

**Why the `(char *)` cast is essential:** pointer arithmetic is scaled by the pointed-to type. `ptr - 16` on a `struct list_node *` would move back 16 × 8 bytes. Casting to `char *` makes the subtraction count single bytes.

**Why kernels use it:** the list code works only with `struct list_node`, so one generic list implementation serves every structure, nodes need no separate allocation, and one object can sit on several lists through several embedded nodes. Linux's `list_head`, `rb_node`, kobjects and almost every driver's private data (`container_of(inode->i_cdev, struct my_dev, cdev)`) rely on it.

**The Linux version adds type checking:** it rejects a `ptr` whose type does not match `member`, using `static_assert` with `__same_type()` (older kernels used a GCC statement expression with `typeof`). The plain version above silently accepts a wrong pointer and returns garbage.

**Follow-up:** the classic way to implement `offsetof` itself is `((size_t)&((type *)0)->member)`. Technically it is undefined behaviour, because it dereferences a null pointer, which is why compilers provide `__builtin_offsetof` and you should use the standard macro.
